UID:stanford-53497842526080@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260902T001324Z
DTSTART:20261111T200000Z
DTEND:20261111T210000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Children and Childhood in Colonial Senegal - Kelly Duke Br
 yant
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/children-and-childhood-in-colonial-se
 negal-kelly-duke-bryant
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/children-and-childhood-in-col
 onial-senegal-kelly-duke-bryant\n報名/票券: https://stanford.zoom.us/w
 ebinar/register/WN_r8SPVFeWQG67NsidVpix1A\n\nHow did French colonial insti
 tutions seek to define\, regulate\, and ‘‘protect’’ childhood in S
 enegal\, and how did children respond? \n\nIn this talk based on her recen
 t book\, Negotiating Childhood: French Colonialism and African Children in
  Senegal\, 1848–1940\, Kelly Duke Bryant explores the lived realities of
  African children across courts\, schools\, streets\, reformatories\, vacc
 ine clinics\, and other sites of colonial intervention. Rather than treati
 ng children as passive subjects\, Bryant illuminates the ways children lim
 ited or negotiated colonial reforms and how the colonial state’s relianc
 e on cooperation produced unexpected forms of resistance and accommodation
 . The talk highlights long-term consequences of colonial governance\, such
  as the privileging of chronological age and the centrality of official do
 cumentation\, that continue to shape Senegalese childhoods.\n\nSpeaker Det
 ails\n\nKelly Duke Bryant is chair of the History Department at Rowan Univ
 ersity\, where she has taught since 2009. She earned her MA in history fro
 m the University of Wisconsin and her Ph.D.\, also in history\, from Johns
  Hopkins University. A specialist in the history of West Africa\, she has 
 published scholarly articles and book chapters on the history of education
 \, childhood\, and youth in colonial Senegal. She is the author of two boo
 ks\, Education as Politics: Colonial Schooling and Political Debate in Sen
 egal\, 1850s-1914 (University of Wisconsin Press\, 2015) and Negotiating C
 hildhood: French Colonialism and
END:VEVENT
