SUMMARY:[現場] Anatomical Gift Program Donor Memorial Service
LOCATION:Memorial Church
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/anatomical-gift-program-donor-memoria
 l-service
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/anatomical-gift-program-donor
 -memorial-service\n報名/票券: https://stanforduniversity.qualtrics.com
 /jfe/form/SV_4Zxiwd4itU47Szc\n\nWe wish to express our deep appreciation f
 or the invaluable contributions each donor has made to our program at Stan
 ford University. Their generosity has left an indelible mark\, touching th
 e lives of countless individuals\, and we come together annually to honor 
 this profound impact through a special memorial service.\n\nIt is with utm
 ost sincerity that we extend a heartfelt invitation to the families of tho
 se donors who entrusted their loved ones to our care between November 2025
  and November 2026 to join us for this significant occasion. We recognize 
 the profound significance of this occasion and warmly welcome you to join 
 us as we gather to honor and cherish the memory of the remarkable individu
 als who have contributed to our program through their selfless gift.\n\nAd
 ditional information regarding logistics and the event's specific details 
 will be communicated to you at a later date. For those unable to attend in
  person\, a recording of the service will be made available for viewing fo
 llowing the event. \n\n \n\nThis event will begin at 6pm and doors will op
 en at 5:30pm.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53691925115269@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T115301Z
DTSTART:20261105T200000Z
DTEND:20261105T210000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Documentary Objectives: Filming Africa from Colonialism to
  Independence - Rachel Gabara
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/documentary-objectives-filming-africa
 -from-colonialism-to-independence-rachel-gabara
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/documentary-objectives-filmin
 g-africa-from-colonialism-to-independence-rachel-gabara\n報名/票券: ht
 tps://forms.gle/DND3whcsRUX6fDyAA\n\nSession Description:\n\nFor well over
  a century\, filmmakers have been shooting documentaries in West and Centr
 al Africa\, a region that included the colonies of French West and Equator
 ial Africa and now encompasses fourteen nations and nearly 200 million peo
 ple. These films played a crucial role in the development of both European
  and African cinemas yet have been largely ignored by scholars. In the wak
 e of fifty years of French colonial documentary in sub-Saharan Africa- pro
 paganda-infused travel\, hunting\, expedition\, and ethnographic films- Af
 rican directors reclaimed their cinematic image by challenging outsider cl
 aims to authenticity and developing new models for nonfiction. I will high
 light the nearly forgotten innovations of the decades following independen
 ce to demonstrate how\, in a complex network of images and languages and a
 cross a dynamic range of modes\, African documentarists remade a global ar
 t form rooted in oppression\, exoticization\, and a simplistic conception 
 of filmic realism.\n\nSpeaker Bio: \n\nRachel Gabara is the Nancy Gillespi
 e Brinning Professor in French at the University of Georgia\, where her te
 aching focuses on nonfiction and historical fiction from Europe and sub-Sa
 haran Africa. In addition to Documentary Objectives\, published earlier th
 is year by Indiana University Press\, Gabara’s recent articles address e
 arly African film criticism (Global Movie Magazine Networks\, University o
 f California Press)\, cinephilia and modernism in African cinema of the la
 te 19
END:VEVENT
