BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53745897362220@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T115718Z
DTSTART:20261030T013000Z
DTEND:20261030T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Witness to Iran’s Paradigm Shift: From Flight PS752 to W
 oman\, Life\, Freedom and Beyond
LOCATION:In person at Stanford
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/witness-to-irans-paradigm-shift-from-
 flight-ps752-to-woman-life-freedom-and-beyond
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/witness-to-irans-paradigm-shi
 ft-from-flight-ps752-to-woman-life-freedom-and-beyond\n報名/票券: http
 s://forms.gle/2ASjNfTJVSUazGfu5\n\nIn this lecture\, Babak Payami argues t
 hat the Woman\, Life\, Freedom uprising was not simply another episode in 
 Iran’s long struggle for freedom. It marked the collapse of a political 
 dialogue with power sustained for decades by repression\, ideological riva
 lry\, and the recurring illusion of reform. A generation born after Ayatol
 lah Khomeini’s death has moved beyond seeking concessions within the Isl
 amic Republic’s framework. Its struggle is increasingly directed at the 
 framework itself\, replacing inherited political and partisan binaries wit
 h a pluralistic language of democratic values\, gender equality\, human di
 gnity\, and justice.\n\nDrawing on his experience as a filmmaker and advoc
 ate working with the families of the victims of Flight PS752\, Payami trac
 es how the language of resistance evolved from cautious reform to direct m
 oral and political rejection. He argues that the families’ campaign for 
 truth and accountability foreshadowed a transformation that had been gathe
 ring force within Iranian civil society for decades. By refusing compensat
 ion as a substitute for truth\, they turned private grief into a disciplin
 ed international campaign and reimagined what organized advocacy for justi
 ce and human rights could look like among Iranians. Inside Iran\, women\, 
 students\, workers\, and historically marginalized communities were challe
 nging both state authority and the legacy ideological divisions that had r
 epeatedly undermined collective action. Digital media made these struggles
  visib
END:VEVENT
