SUMMARY:[現場] Elamite Art Through the Lens of Archaeological Evidence
LOCATION:In person at Stanford
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/elamite-art-through-the-lens-of-archa
 eological-evidence
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/elamite-art-through-the-lens-
 of-archaeological-evidence\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/XFeAWe3ue3Co2
 h4X7\n\nEvent is in Persian.\n\nProfessor Bahram Ghadiri examines Elamite 
 art as a major body of archaeological evidence for understanding the cultu
 ral\, political\, and religious development of southwestern Iran and the b
 roader Iranian Plateau. Rather than approaching Elamite art primarily as a
  collection of isolated aesthetic objects\, the presentation situates arti
 stic production within its historical\, geographical\, and social contexts
 .\n\nThe lecture begins with an overview of the emergence of Elamite polit
 ical formations and their changing territorial extent from the late fourth
  millennium to the first millennium BCE. It considers the relationships am
 ong important centers such as Susa\, Anshan\, Haft Tappeh\, Chogha Zanbil\
 , and the highland regions associated with Elamite cultural traditions.\n\
 nThe second section addresses the principal methodological challenges invo
 lved in interpreting Elamite art. Particular attention is given to archaeo
 logical context\, chronology\, iconography\, inscriptions\, patterns of cu
 ltural exchange\, and the limitations created by incomplete or displaced e
 vidence. The lecture also considers how earlier scholarly frameworks have 
 influenced modern interpretations of Elamite artistic identity.\n\nThe fin
 al section examines selected examples of monumental architecture\, rock re
 liefs\, seals\, sculpture\, metalwork\, ceramics\, and representations of 
 music and ritual. These materials illuminate concepts of kingship\, religi
 ous practice\, social organization\, collective memory\, and
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53808344432557@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T120412Z
DTSTART:20261104T230000Z
DTEND:20261105T012000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Digital Mirrors of Materiality
LOCATION:Anderson Collection\, Denning Family Resource Center
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/digital-mirrors-of-materiality
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/digital-mirrors-of-materialit
 y\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSf9oEQbuYA6Q3Od
 ZVyqOpz8lMLeu97FIc3o0pjMiFq3_rl_fQ/viewform?usp=header\n\npart of *Defying
  Boundaries: MacArthur Fellows at 45\n\nwith Katherine Behar\, Nick Benson
 *\, Hideo Mabuchi*\, and Camille Utterback*\nModerated by Vanessa Chang\n\
 nNovember 4\, 3:00-5:20PM\n\nDenning Family Resource Center\, Anderson Col
 lection\n\nReception to follow\n5:30-6:30PM at the McMurtry Building Court
 yard\n\nWe are all in some way navigating widespread futureshock over para
 digm-breaking growth in the power of computation and robotics. Stepping ba
 ck from the most expansive societal stakes to seek grounding at personal s
 cale\, expressive making offers a rich context within which to reflect upo
 n what craft does for the maker\, and what is lost or gained when we augme
 nt or supplant embodied engagement with digital technologies. If our goal 
 is not just to produce but to make things as a way of being at home in the
  world\, what could we possibly want from the leverage of fabulous machine
 s? Can traditional craft assimilate the expansiveness of the digital? How 
 does this historical moment call for us to affirm the value of practicing 
 heirloom modes of creativity?\n\n\nLearn more and RSVP\n****\n\nThis event
  made possible by John D. and Catherine T. MacArthur Foundation \, Funding
  Good Chaos\, Stanford Arts Institute (SAI)\, the Department of Art and Ar
 t History  and the Office of the Vice President for the Arts.
END:VEVENT
