BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53852108956001@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260831T233821Z
DTSTART:20261112T013000Z
DTEND:20261112T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] BOOK LAUNCH: Literature and Emergency Law in Kashmir and N
 orthern Ireland
LOCATION:Building 80\, Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-launch-literature-and-emergency-
 law-in-kashmir-and-northern-ireland
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-launch-literature-and-em
 ergency-law-in-kashmir-and-northern-ireland\n報名/票券: https://docs.g
 oogle.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdQah3_uTR1wJVo42_g9NlrI24aytDewDVEZUSI91E2EPRH
 xg/viewform\n\nThe Department of African and African American Studies (DAA
 AS) in collaboration with the Center for South Asia (CSA) cordially invite
  the Stanford Community for a special book launch!\n\nWe are excited to ce
 lebrate the publication of a new book by our visiting postdoctoral scholar
 \, Danny Shanhan titled\, Literature and Emergency Law in Kashmir and Nort
 hern Ireland.\n\nThis book is the first substantial study of emergency law
 's relationship to literature in either the Northern Irish or the Kashmiri
  conflict and develops an original legal history that links these laws to 
 a shared British colonial root. Engaging a wide range of fiction\, poetry 
 and film\, from canonical poets Seamus Heaney and Agha Shahid Ali to conte
 mporary works by Anna Burns\, Mirza Waheed\, and Bollywood filmmaker Visha
 l Bhardwaj\, the book examines the literary response to the suspension of 
 normative legal rights such as habeas corpus\, the right to silence and ev
 en\, in Kashmir\, the right to life. By bringing these literary cultures t
 ogether for the first time\, it develops a literary and legal history that
  reveals common roots in colonial-era jurisprudence. The book re-situates 
 these corpora\, often isolated in scholarly work\, as important parts of t
 he global\, material legacies of colonialism.\n\nJoin us for an evening of
  conversation\, community and celebration and Danny shares his work.\n\nRe
 freshments will be provided.\n\nPlease RSVP here.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53853306172764@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260922T010602Z
DTSTART:20261204T200000Z
DTEND:20261204T200000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Who’s Afraid of the East Romans?
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/whos-afraid-of-the-east-romans
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/whos-afraid-of-the-east-roman
 s\n\nRecent years have seen a push to recognize the east Roman state and i
 ts people for what and who they said they were — Romans — and not to h
 ide that reality under the invented term “Byzantium\,” which has alway
 s encoded negative stereotypes. In this talk\, professor Kaldellis will di
 scuss the ideologies and structural interests that have resisted acknowled
 ging a Roman people in the east\, ranging from western Eurocentrism to mod
 ern Greek nationalism. “Rome" has long been a foundational matrix for th
 inking about western identities and states: what would it look like if it 
 made room to accommodate its eastern branch on an equal basis?\n\nRSVP her
 e.\n\nAnthony Kaldellis is a professor of Classics at the University of Ch
 icago. He has published widely on the history\, culture\, and literature o
 f Byzantium\, ranging from the fourth to the fifteenth centuries. His work
 s include a new\, comprehensive history of the eastern Roman empire: The N
 ew Roman Empire: A History of Byzantium (Oxford\, 2023). Most recently he 
 has published a new history of the fall of Constantinople in 1453 and a st
 udy of western perceptions of Byzantium from late antiquity to World War I
 I (Phantom Byzantium). He has translated medieval Greek authors\, includin
 g historians such as Prokopios\, Attaleiates\, and Laonikos Chalkokondyles
 \, and is the host of the academic podcast “Byzantium & Friends\,” goi
 ng strong since 2019.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53853336941939@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260831T234945Z
DTSTART:20270211T170000Z
DTEND:20270211T230000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stanford Global and Planetary Health Research Convening
LOCATION:Frances C. Arrillaga Alumni Center Stanford\, CA
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/stanford-global-and-planetary-health-
 research-convening
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/stanford-global-and-planetary
 -health-research-convening\n報名/票券: https://www.eventbrite.com/e/20
 27-stanford-global-and-planetary-health-research-convening-tickets-1998392
 877893?aff=oddtdtcreator\n\nAbout the Stanford Global and Planetary Health
  Research Convening\n\nThe 2027 Global and Planetary Health Research Conve
 ning will take place Thursday\, Feb. 11 from 9am – 3pm at Stanford.\n\nT
 he Annual Global and Planetary Health Research Convening fosters discussio
 n among researchers across a variety of disciplines. We welcome participan
 ts to share their ideas and experiences with global and planetary health r
 esearch and to explore new collaborations and opportunities for engagement
 . Prior convenings have attracted broad participation from physician-scien
 tists\, engineers\, economists\, earth scientists\, ecologists\, and mathe
 matical modelers.\n\nRegister here\n\nAbstract Submissions\n\nThe Stanford
  Center for Human and Planetary Health and the Center for Innovation in Gl
 obal Health invite everyone wishing to report on original research or inte
 rventions to submit abstracts to be considered for presentation. This year
 ’s theme is Global and Planetary Health. We welcome abstracts from a bro
 ad range of topics and disciplines related to these topics. Apply below by
  Oct. 22 at 5pm PT. \n\nNote: Abstracts should be no longer than 1700 char
 acters and must represent academic research.\n\nIf you have any questions\
 , please contact Yosefa Gilon at ygilon@stanford.edu.\n\nSubmit an abstrac
 t
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53853582945229@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260911T052904Z
DTSTART:20270218T040000Z
DTEND:20270218T053000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Reading with Robin Coste Lewis\, the Mohr Visiting Poet
LOCATION:Faculty Club\, Cedar Room
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/reading-with-robin-coste-lewis-the-mo
 hr-visiting-poet
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/reading-with-robin-coste-lewi
 s-the-mohr-visiting-poet\n\nThe Creative Writing Program is pleased to ann
 ounce the next event with the Mohr Visiting Poet: A Reading with Robin Cos
 te Lewis.\n\nThis event is open to Stanford affiliates and the general pub
 lic. Registration is encouraged but not required\; RSVP form to follow.\n\
 n____\n\nRobin Coste Lewis is a writer and artist. Lewis won the National 
 Book Award for Voyage of the Sable Venus\, her first collection of poe
 try. The book was also a finalist for the Hurston/Wright Legacy Award and 
 the Los Angeles Times Book Award\, and it was named one of the best bo
 oks of the year by The New Yorker and The New York Times. Literary H
 ub named it one of the best books of the last twenty years. Lewis was th
 e first African-American to ever win the National Book Award in a debut\, 
 and the first woman to do so since 1974. Her second book\, To the Realiz
 ation of Perfect Helplessness\, was the winner of the PEN/Voelcker Award f
 or Poetry and the NAACP Image Award for Outstanding Literary Work in Poetr
 y. She is also the coauthor\, with Kevin Young\, of Robert Rauschenberg:
  Thirty-Four Illustrations for Dante’s Inferno\, published by the Muse
 um of Modern Art. Her latest book\, Archive of Desire\, was named by the
  Brooklyn Public Library as one of the best poetry collections of the year
 . The former poet laureate of Los Angeles\, Lewis holds a PhD in poetry an
 d visual studies from the University of Southern California\, an MFA in po
 etry from New York University\, an MTS in Sanskrit and comparative religio
 us literature from t
END:VEVENT
