UID:stanford-53862757496054@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260904T105640Z
DTSTART:20270129T010000Z
DTEND:20270129T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Anning Jing: "Borobudur and a Tantric Buddhist Maṇḍala
  Associated with Vajrabodhi (671–741)"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/anning-jing-borobudur-and-a-tantric-b
 uddhist-maala-associated-with-vajrabodhi-671-741
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/anning-jing-borobudur-and-a-t
 antric-buddhist-maala-associated-with-vajrabodhi-671-741\n\nAbstract:\n\nT
 his talk reexamines Borobudur through the transmission of Esoteric Buddhis
 t teachings associated with Indian Tantric Buddhist master Vajrabodhi (671
 –741). It argues that Vajrabodhi’s activities in Java and China\, his 
 transmission of Tantric teachings to Amoghavajra\, and the subsequent init
 iation of the Javanese monk Bianhong into the Garbhadhātu Maṇḍala by 
 Amoghavajra’s disciple Huiguo created the historical and doctrinal frame
 work for Borobudur’s construction. A three-dimensional Buddhist maṇḍ
 ala known as the shi 式 or “secret disc”\, described in a Tang Buddhi
 st text attributed to Vajrabodhi\, closely parallels Borobudur’s archite
 ctural form\, cosmological model\, iconographic program of the Five Jina B
 uddhas\, and solar symbolism. It proposes that Borobudur was conceived as 
 a monumental celestial maṇḍala of Mahāvairocana\, shaped by Vajrabodh
 i’s Esoteric Buddhist lineage and its cosmological vision. Vajrabodhi’
 s shi\, however\, was rooted in the traditional Chinese shi\, whose origin
 s can be traced to the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture of southeastern China.\n
 \nBio:\n\nAnning Jing received his MA in Art History from Ohio State Unive
 rsity in 1989 and PhD in Art History from Princeton University in 1993. He
  has been teaching Asian art at Michigan State University since 1995. His 
 main publications include The Water God's Temple of the Guangsheng Monaste
 ry: Cosmic Function of Art\, Ritual\, and Theater (Leiden: Brill\, 2002)\,
  Yuandai biha “Shenxianfuhui tu” (The Yuan Murals “Assembly o
END:VEVENT
