SUMMARY:[現場] Urban Studies Model Scholar Lecture | How Surprising Neigh
 borhood Changes Are Bringing Down Black-White Segregation
LOCATION:Building 120\, McClatchy Hall\, Main Quad\, Studio 40
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/urban-studies-20th-annual-model-schol
 ar-lecturehow-surprising-neighborhood-changes-are-bringing-down-black-whit
 e-segregation
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/urban-studies-20th-annual-mod
 el-scholar-lecturehow-surprising-neighborhood-changes-are-bringing-down-bl
 ack-white-segregation\n\nJoin Urban Studies for the 20th Annual Model Scho
 lar Lecture featuring John R. Logan\, sociology professor at Brown Univers
 ity\n\nAbout the talk: It is well known by now that the overall level of B
 lack-white segregation in American urban areas is going down.  One surpris
 e is that the largest contributions to this decline are coming from predom
 inantly Black neighborhoods that are remaining very disproportionately Bla
 ck.  To a large extent these are the poor inner city neighborhoods that we
 re built up by the Great Migration in the mid-20th Century era of near-apa
 rtheid.  Their Black residents are leaving them in large numbers and not b
 eing replaced by whites or other groups.  In itself this exodus is large e
 nough to have a powerful deconcentrating effect.  But the second surprise 
 is what is happening in the rest of the metropolis.  Neighborhood changes 
 that lower segregation by increasing racial diversity in neighborhoods are
  outweighed by counter-trends in the opposite direction.  Most striking\, 
 the positive effect of Black growth in established white neighborhoods -- 
 a new and positive trend\, to be sure -- is more than counterbalanced by t
 he continuing Black growth in a different set of Black neighborhoods.  If 
 this is the end of a century of segregation\, as some have described it\, 
 it is arriving with many of the old patterns still in place.\n\nJohn R. Lo
 gan completed his PhD in Sociology at the University of California\, Berke
 ley\, in 1974. Before coming to Brown he was Distinguish
END:VEVENT
