SUMMARY:[現場] Book Event with Roland Betancourt and T.L. Taylor\, "Disne
 yland and the Rise of Automation"
LOCATION:Green Library\, Bing Wing\, 122
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-event-with-roland-betancourt-and
 -tl-taylor-disneyland-and-the-rise-of-automation
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-event-with-roland-betanc
 ourt-and-tl-taylor-disneyland-and-the-rise-of-automation\n報名/票券: h
 ttps://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdIh4Oe5gc56WhNKyXZ0kvVtQiTgrdns8_
 WbXFBcnrHAQGhDQ/viewform?usp=publish-editor\n\nPlease join the Silicon Val
 ley Archives in welcoming Roland Betancourt and T.L. Taylor as they discus
 s Betancourt's latest book\, Disneyland and Rise of Automation\, on Decemb
 er 2\, 2026\, at 4:30 p.m. \n\n \n\nDisneyland and the Rise of Automation 
 argues that theme park rides are factory assembly lines in disguise\, ones
  that manufacture subjects rather than products. The book traces the techn
 ological systems that made this possible\, from limit switches and relay r
 acks to programmable logic controllers and magnetic tape\, revealing how i
 nnovations developed for wartime manufacturing and Cold War industry found
  new life in the Happiest Place on Earth. It applies insights from the his
 tory of technology to problems shared by historians\, media scholars\, and
  critics of visual culture\, such as the aesthetics of automation and the 
 politics of labor.\n\n  \n\nRoland Betancourt is a Chancellor’s Professo
 r at the University of California\, Irvine. He is currently the Andrew W. 
 Mellon Professor at the Center for Advanced Study in the Visual Arts at th
 e National Gallery of Art. A 2023 Guggenheim Fellow\, he has published boo
 ks on Byzantine visual culture\, gender\, and sexuality\, including the pr
 ize-winning Byzantine Intersectionality (Princeton\, 2020).\n\n \n\nT.L. T
 aylor is Professor of Comparative Media Studies at MITand affiliate facult
 y member in Anthropology and Women’s and Gender Studies. She is a qualit
 ative sociologist who has focused on the interrelations between culture an
 d technology in online en
END:VEVENT
