UID:stanford-53976866896468@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T164655Z
DTSTART:20261119T013000Z
DTEND:20261119T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Disappearance of Enforced Disappearances in Kashmir: M
 ilitarization\, Mourning\, and the Production of Normalcy
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-disappearance-of-enforced-disappe
 arances-in-kashmir-militarization-mourning-and-the-production-of-normalcy-
 9170
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-disappearance-of-enforced
 -disappearances-in-kashmir-militarization-mourning-and-the-production-of-n
 ormalcy-9170\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/LFswcLeYLkkXfHJfA\n\nThis e
 vent is co-sponsored by the Center for South Asia \, the Abbasi Program in
  Islamic Studies \, and the Department of Anthropology.\n\nAbout the speak
 er\nAther Zia\, PhD\, is a political anthropologist\, poet\, short fiction
  writer\, and columnist. She is a Professor of Anthropology and Gender Stu
 dies at the University of Northern Colorado\, Greeley. She is the author o
 f Resisting Disappearances: Military Occupation and Women’s Activism in 
 Kashmir (University of Washington Press\, 2019\; Zubaan Books South Asia e
 dition\, 2020). She is the co-editor of Can You Hear Kashmiri Women Speak?
  (Women Unlimited\, 2020)\, Resisting Occupation in Kashmir (University of
  Pennsylvania Press\, 2018)\, and A Desolation Called Peace (HarperCollins
 \, 2019). Her recent poetry collection In Kashmir: Writing Under Occupatio
 n was published by Agitate Collective. She is the founder-editor of Kashmi
 r Lit\, and co-founder of the Critical Kashmir Studies Collective. Ather i
 s also a former co-editor of Cultural Anthropology (2022–2025) and Assoc
 iate Editor of the Encyclopedia of Women & Islamic Cultures.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53977038029919@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260914T232356Z
DTSTART:20261106T003000Z
DTEND:20261106T020000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Fate of the Social in Modern Life: American Social Sci
 entists and Japan as Archive
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-fate-of-the-social-in-modern-life
 -american-social-scientists-and-japan-as-archive
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-fate-of-the-social-in-mod
 ern-life-american-social-scientists-and-japan-as-archive\n報名/票券: h
 ttps://luma.com/5ach1gu9\n\nIn the decades following World War II\, Americ
 an social scientists turned to the study of Japanese society as an emergin
 g ally and fellow capitalist democracy. At a time when influential theorie
 s of modernization predicted the decline of kinship\, community\, hierarch
 y\, and collective obligation\, Japan presented a puzzle. It was an advanc
 ed industrial and capitalist society in which village\, family\, corporate
  welfare\, and moral persuasion in public institutions played powerful rol
 es in organizing everyday life.\n\n​This talk examines how postwar schol
 ars treated Japan not simply as an object of study but as a site for think
 ing through a larger problem in modern social thought: the relationship be
 tween individual freedom and social solidarity. Drawing on chapters from a
  larger book project\, I revisit three influential studies of Japan: Ruth 
 Benedict's The Chrysanthemum and the Sword (1946)\, Ezra Vogel's Japan's N
 ew Middle Class (1963)\, and Thomas Rohlen's For Harmony and Strength (197
 4). I argue that these works are best understood not only as interpretatio
 ns of Japan but also as reflections on what I call the "social question" i
 n postwar thought: how modern societies cultivate attachment\, obligation\
 , welfare\, and collective purpose while preserving democratic freedoms an
 d individual autonomy. By placing these texts in conversation\, the talk s
 uggests that postwar Japan functioned as a distinctive archive for thinkin
 g about enduring questions of social reproduction and social solida
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53977460472650@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T121523Z
DTSTART:20261104T180000Z
DTEND:20261104T190000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] CSRE PhD Minor Info Session
LOCATION:Building 360\, CCSRE Conference Room\, Room 361J
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/csre-phd-minor-info-session-3203
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/csre-phd-minor-info-session-3
 203\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfbwzLrPW4WQI
 JwOfCtNP6reVLBJYtLPEk-gaElAg5nqVLQ9w/viewform?usp=sharing&ouid=10630757281
 7847635960\n\nAre you interested in pursuing a graduate certificate in rac
 e and ethnic studies? \n\nFind out more about the PhD Minor in Comparative
  Studies in Race and Ethnicity (CSRE)! \n\nJoin us over pastries and coffe
 e/tea to learn more about coursework\, degree requirements\, the declarati
 on process\, and the PhD Minor community. Program leadership and current P
 hD Minors will be on hand to answer all your questions. \n\nRSVP HERE!!!\n
 \nFeel free to reach out to CCSRE Executive Director\, Annie Atura\, at at
 ura@stanford.edu with any questions about the event or about CCSRE graduat
 e programming. \n\nYou can also learn more about the PhD Minor program her
 e and browse profiles of current PhD Minors here.\n\nWe hope you can join 
 us! Spread the word\; all are welcome.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53985474151662@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T192427Z
DTSTART:20261028T010000Z
DTEND:20261028T010000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Offline Dream Program: Three Takes on Film\, the Inter
 net\, and Instanternity
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-offline-dream-program-three-takes
 -on-film-the-internet-and-instanternity
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-offline-dream-program-thr
 ee-takes-on-film-the-internet-and-instanternity\n\nAs part of their Repeti
 tion and Contingency series\, held across U.S. universities\, Bara Kolenc\
 , Gregor Moder\, and Jure Simoniti (Department of Philosophy at the Univer
 sity of Ljubljana) explore pressing questions concerning the shifting rela
 tionship between media\, reality\, and human agency. In today’s age\, wh
 en artificial intelligence dreams of cinema and the internet shapes our de
 sires as so many permutations of ideology\, the panel asks: Where is subje
 ctivity to be located and what functions does it perform? This panel is mo
 derated by Pavle Levi\, Department of Art & Art History\, Stanford Univers
 ity.\n\nRSVP here.\n\nPresentation Abstracts\nLubitsch\, Shakespeare and t
 he Theatricality of Power\, Gregor Moder\nErnst Lubitsch’s To Be or Not 
 to Be (1942) was filmed during World War II and takes place in the early p
 eriod of the Nazi occupation of Poland\, yet the film focuses on the story
  of a theatre group\, on actors\, and on the metaphysical question of what
  makes up a convincing performance. Some early critics suggested that this
  is not the way to tackle a dire political situation\, and that the portra
 yal of Nazis as humans\, with their own sense of humor and theatre\, was d
 isrespectful to the plight of the Poles and Polish Jewry. For the film\, h
 owever\, the political action and the metaphysical implications of a theat
 rical performance are not alternative procedures\, but are rather closely 
 linked to one another\, and in this respect Lubitsch follows Shakespeare
 ’s own staging of power. The fi
END:VEVENT
