SUMMARY:[混合] Improbable Research and the Ig Nobel Prizes
LOCATION:Li Ka Shing Center\, Berg Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/medicine-grand-rounds-with-marc-abrah
 ams
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/medicine-grand-rounds-with-ma
 rc-abrahams\n線上資訊:\nAccess dial in details at https://medicine.st
 anford.edu/education/grand-rounds.html\n\nStanford Department of Medicine 
 would like to invite all Stanford community members to attend this instanc
 e of the Medicine Grand Rounds (MGR) series\, which will feature Marc Abra
 hams\, the founder of the Ig Nobel Prize\, awarded annually to research th
 at "first makes you laugh\, then makes you think\," and the editor of Anna
 ls of Improbable Research\, whose career is dedicated to celebrating scien
 ce at its most surprising and delightfully absurd.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53994605586594@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T121646Z
DTSTART:20261105T023000Z
DTEND:20261105T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stegner Fellow Reading with Daniel Lurie and Katerina Ivan
 ov Prado
LOCATION:Building 460\, Margaret Jacks Hall\, Terrace Room (Room 426)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/stegner-fellow-reading-with-daniel-lu
 rie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/stegner-fellow-reading-with-d
 aniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.
 com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSe_0ZHag7NlCSlcVCuDITkqir4iH2g6MANsXlu_Md7xQDm6_w/vie
 wform?usp=header\n\nThe Creative Writing Program is pleased to announce a 
 reading with Stegner Fellows in Poetry and Fiction: Daniel Lurie and Kater
 ina Ivanov Prado. \n\nThis event is open to Stanford affiliates and the ge
 neral public. Registration is encouraged but not required. Register here\n
 \n____\n\nDaniel Lurie is a Jewish\, rural writer\, from eastern Montana. 
 He holds an MFA in Poetry from the University of Idaho and is co-editor of
  Outskirts Literary Journal. His work has appeared or is forthcoming in Pl
 eiades\, Gulf Coast\, RHINO\, Adroit\, The Iowa Review\, Poetry Northwest\
 , and other publications. Daniel won the 2026 Mississippi Review Prize\, P
 inch’s Literary Award\, and phoebe’s Greg Grummer Poetry Contest\, sel
 ected by Diane Seuss. He served as the 2025-2026 Ronald Wallace Poetry Fel
 low at the University of Wisconsin-Madison. Find him at danielluriepoetry.
 com \n\nKaterina Ivanov Prado received an MFA from the University of Arizo
 na and a Ph.D. in Literature and Creative Writing from the University of H
 ouston. Her work has appeared in Narrative\, Brevity\, Catapult\, The Rump
 us\, and Joyland\, and has won The Masters Review Emerging Fiction Award\,
  the Narrative Story Contest\, and the AWP Intro Journals Award\, as well 
 as received support from the Elizabeth George Foundation\, VCCA\, Inprint 
 Houston\, Reese’s Book Club\, and the Bread Loaf Writers Conference.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53994806337231@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260917T031408Z
DTSTART:20270128T023000Z
DTEND:20270128T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stegner Fellow Reading with Cheyenne Fletcher and Yi Jiang
LOCATION:Building 460\, Margaret Jacks Hall\, Terrace Room (Room 426)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-reading-with-d
 aniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-readin
 g-with-daniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado\n報名/票券: https://docs
 .google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdPCA9-27GQk3xyRX0XcQCtULFSFTsQeCy2W6ObXVcueJ
 4QzQ/viewform?usp=header\n\nThe Creative Writing Program is pleased to ann
 ounce a reading with Stegner Fellows in Poetry and Fiction: Cheyenne Fletc
 her and Yi Jiang. \n\nThis event is open to Stanford affiliates and the ge
 neral public. Registration is encouraged but not required. Register here\n
 \n____\n\nCheyenne C. Fletcher (she/they) is a poet\, educator\, auntie\, 
 and citizen of the Cherokee Nation. She is the recipient of Shō Poetry Jo
 urnal’s Sita Martin Prize and a 2026 Best New Poets nominee. She receive
 d her MFA from the University of San Francisco.\n\nYi Jiang is a writer fr
 om Shanghai and Wisconsin. Her fiction has appeared or is forthcoming in P
 loughshares\, The Missouri Review\, Gulf Coast\, and elsewhere. She comple
 ted her MFA at Vanderbilt University\, and has received support from Bread
  Loaf Writers' Conference. She previously worked as an associate editor at
  BookPage and a systems engineer in healthcare technology\, prior to a car
 eer shift.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53995111265657@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260917T031824Z
DTSTART:20270211T023000Z
DTEND:20270211T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stegner Fellow Reading with Priscilla Wathington and Jonat
 hon Atkinson
LOCATION:Building 460\, Margaret Jacks Hall\, Terrace Room (Room 426)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-reading-with-c
 heyenne-fletcher-and-yi-jiang
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-readin
 g-with-cheyenne-fletcher-and-yi-jiang\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.
 com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSc9BSvBZUtnyfX6KWy8eXLgjupKcZOMXbi9Hd7K0Omw3iJLBQ/vie
 wform?usp=dialog\n\nThe Creative Writing Program is pleased to announce a 
 reading with Stegner Fellows in Poetry and Fiction: Priscilla Wathington a
 nd Jonathon Atkinson. \n\nThis event is open to Stanford affiliates and th
 e general public. Registration is encouraged but not required. Register he
 re\n\n____\n\nPriscilla Wathington is a Palestinian American poet and the 
 author of the chapbook\, "Paper and Stick". Her poems have appeared in The
  Adroit Journal\, Four Way Review\, Prairie Schooner\, Michigan Quarterly 
 Review\, and elsewhere. She was named a Djanikian Scholar in 2026 and a Ti
 n House Scholar in 2025\, and has previously served as a Sam Mazza Writer 
 in Residence at San Francisco State University’s Poetry Center. Wathingt
 on is a Senior Poetry Editor at Harbor Review and holds an MFA from Warren
  Wilson College.\n\nJonathon Atkinson is a writer from Northern California
 . His work has appeared in n+1\, New Ohio Review\, and Water~Stone Review.
  He holds an MFA from the University of Minnesota\, Twin Cities\, teaches 
 in the Language and Thinking Program at Bard College\, and mentors with th
 e Minnesota Prison Writing Workshop.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53995193396427@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260917T031952Z
DTSTART:20270513T013000Z
DTEND:20270513T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stegner Fellow Reading with J.L. Chen and Heidi Kaloustian
LOCATION:Stanford ChEM-H Building\, John A. and Cynthia Fry Gunn Rotunda\, 
 E241
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-reading-with-d
 aniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado-7530
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-readin
 g-with-daniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado-7530\n報名/票券: https:/
 /docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSe_cyQG7aKsG3uFaiS_fhqIS4Pa3NA3fd58ZEaP4
 NWJwCRtvw/viewform?usp=header\n\nThe Creative Writing Program is pleased t
 o announce a reading with Stegner Fellows in Poetry and Fiction: J.L. Chen
  and Heidi Kaloustian. \n\nThis event is open to Stanford affiliates and t
 he general public. Registration is encouraged but not required. Register h
 ere\n\n____\n\nJ.L. Chen was born in Shanghai\, lived in New York for near
 ly a decade before making her home in Vancouver\, Canada\, where she write
 s most mornings and dances into the night. Working in both English and Chi
 nese\, she has published poetry and personal essays in ARC Poetry\, Litera
 ry Review of Canada\, Tupelo Quarterly\, Northwest Review\, PRISM Internat
 ional\, Queen's Quarterly\, subTerrain\, and more. A finalist for the 2025
  Robert and Adele Schiff Award (Cincinnati Review) and longlisted for the 
 2025 CBC Poetry Prize\, Chen holds an MFA from the University of King's Co
 llege and is at work on a poetry manuscript and a memoir. (Instagram @jlch
 enwrites)\n\nHeidi Kaloustian is a writer from Michigan. She holds an MFA 
 from the Iowa Writers' Workshop\, where she was the Fiction Editor of the 
 literary journal The Iowa Review. She has been the recipient of an Avery H
 opwood Award in Fiction\, a Kresge Fellowship in the Literary Arts\, and a
  Robert J. Schulze Memorial Fellowship for a first novel. She is currently
  at work on a novel and a collection of stories.
END:VEVENT
