 gle.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeJHK7-F0NY4_nDRk5z747dSeSqISKyzhxy6aT5Y-MwQ5TATA
 /viewform\n\nJoin the Other Europe Research Workshop in hosting Dr. Erica 
 Camisa Morale (Slavic Languages & Literatures) for a talk entitled From Ya
 hweh to Jupiter: Orthodox Muscovy Deals with the Classical World. \n\nCons
 idering a few instances taking place between 1680–1705\, the talk shows 
 how interculturalism\, multilingualism\, and multiconfessionalism in the P
 olish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and in Ruthenia helped to spread Classical c
 ulture into Eastern Europe. In his collection\, Multiflowered Garden (1680
 )\, the Belarusian Simiaon Polacki breaks with Orthodox skepticism towards
  ancient culture—deemed as sophistic—and includes references to Classi
 cal literature and mythology as signs of erudition and stylistic refinemen
 t to place the Slavic world in a line of continuity with the ancient one. 
 The Polish-by-birth and Muscovite-by-adoption Jan Białobocki dedicates th
 e fifth book of Pentateugum (1690s) to Classical history and mythology\, b
 ut depicts them negatively as symbols of vanity and cause of disruption. I
 n the early eighteenth century\, the Ukrainian Teofan Prokopovych states i
 n his De arte poetica that poets should not reference Classical culture in
  their lyrics as it is foreign to the East Slavic world\, which is rooted 
 in Orthodoxy. Yet\, Teofan’s poems abound in references to Classical myt
 hology and play a key role in introducing Neoclassicism into the Russian E
 mpire. These instances show a process at play\, namely the reception of an
 cient culture in Muscovy\, and the importance of Belaru
END:VEVENT
