BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54003952174869@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T121734Z
DTSTART:20261105T023000Z
DTEND:20261105T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] You Belong Here: A Student Workshop
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/you-belong-here-a-student-workshop
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/you-belong-here-a-student-wor
 kshop\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/rYtTG5MQq8oxoR7A8\n\nYou Belong He
 re: A Student Workshop creates a supportive\, reflective space for Black a
 nd African students to explore belonging\, build confidence\, and strength
 en connections across campus. Through guided conversation\, small-group re
 flection\, and brief skills practice\, participants will surface common ex
 periences of self-doubt and imposter feelings while recognizing the accomp
 lishments and resilience that brought them to Stanford.\n\nFacilitators wi
 ll introduce practical strategies for reframing negative self-talk\, celeb
 rating milestones\, and cultivating peer networks that sustain academic an
 d personal well-being. The session is intentionally participant-centered: 
 attendees can choose their level of engagement\, and confidentiality and m
 utual respect are emphasized.\n\nFaculty and staff are invited to attend i
 n a supportive role to listen\, learn\, and reinforce institutional commit
 ment to inclusive belonging\; their presence should prioritize students’
  voices and experiences. Whether you are seeking reassurance\, connection\
 , or concrete tools to bolster confidence\, this workshop offers a welcomi
 ng environment to reaffirm that you earned your place at Stanford and belo
 ng in this community.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54004011507794@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T121734Z
DTSTART:20261029T190000Z
DTEND:20261029T201500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Benjamin Yoel — International Pressure and Opposition to
  Democratic Backsliding
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/benjamin-yoel-international-pressure-
 and-opposition-to-democratic-backsliding
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/benjamin-yoel-international-p
 ressure-and-opposition-to-democratic-backsliding\n線上連結: https://st
 anford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nUnder
  what conditions do pro-democracy global actors support domestic oppositio
 n actors resisting democratic backsliding\, and when does such support sha
 pe public opinion? This book project develops a theory of international rh
 etorical pressure that examines both the supply and demand of foreign enga
 gement in backsliding contexts. On the supply side\, it argues that intern
 ational actors' public attention to democratic developments abroad is shap
 ed by both domestic political developments and geopolitical ties. On the d
 emand side\, it suggests that foreign statements can shape public opinion 
 toward pro-democracy movements. Drawing on an original dataset of US Secre
 tary of State speeches\, survey experiments\, and elite interviews\, the p
 roject shows that geopolitical interests often shape when international ac
 tors publicly address democracy abroad\, but that when they do\, foreign m
 essages can meaningfully shape public opinion. The project highlights the 
 increasingly international nature of backsliding and both the opportunitie
 s and challenges international engagement creates for domestic opposition 
 actors.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nBenjamin Yoel is the Jan Koum Israel Studi
 es Program Postdoctoral Fellow at Stanford University’s Center on Democr
 acy\, Development and the Rule of Law (CDDRL). Benjamin studies democratic
  backsliding\, nonviolent resistance\, authoritarian politics\, and the ro
 le of international factors in the context of regime change. He is particu
 larly interested in understanding
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54004031461183@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T121734Z
DTSTART:20261105T200000Z
DTEND:20261105T211500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Marcela Ibáñez — Authoritarian Aid and Democratic Atti
 tudes: Backlash\, Not Diffusion
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/marcela-ibanez-cddrl-research-seminar
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/marcela-ibanez-cddrl-research
 -seminar\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfR
 GRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nHow does the local presence of an authoritari
 an power shape citizens' commitment to democracy? Existing theories sugges
 t engagement with authoritarian states should weaken democratic commitment
  through norm diffusion\, accommodation\, or authoritarian learning. This 
 paper challenges that expectation by examining Chinese development finance
  in Latin America. Using geocoded data\, it estimates the subnational effe
 ct of exposure to Chinese aid on democratic attitudes. Exposure increases 
 support for democracy rather than undermining it. The effect is strongest 
 in authoritarian and weakly democratic settings and absent in consolidated
  democracies. Exposure does not improve perceptions of China or the United
  States\, casting doubt on soft power or foreign-comparison explanations. 
 Instead\, the paper argues that visible Chinese projects make incumbents' 
 authoritarian character more salient\, triggering an accountability respon
 se. These findings recast authoritarian external influence as a contingent
  catalyst for democratic reinforcement.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nMarcela Ib
 áñez is a Visiting Postdoctoral Fellow at CDDRL and is a Postdoctoral Re
 searcher at the Chair of Political Economy and Development at the Universi
 ty of Zurich. She holds a Ph.D. in Political Science from the University o
 f Mannheim. Her work lies at the intersection of political economy\, devel
 opment\, and vulnerability reduction. Her research interests include inequ
 ality in low and middle-income economies\, sustainability and de
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54004063149104@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260929T002315Z
DTSTART:20261112T200000Z
DTEND:20261112T211500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Jeanet Sinding Bentzen — Risky Business? Religiosity and
  Risk-Taking in Full-Population Danish Data
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/jeanet-sinding-bentzen-cddrl-research
 -seminar
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/jeanet-sinding-bentzen-cddrl-
 research-seminar\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd
 =x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nWhy do individuals differ so persiste
 ntly in their willingness to take economic risks? This paper investigates 
 whether religiosity\, particularly the intensity of religious belief\, and
  helps explain this variation. The research considers two competing theore
 tical predictions: religious teachings discourage immoral and unlawful beh
 avior\, while the religious coping literature suggests that belief in a be
 nevolent God reduces background anxiety and effectively provides psycholog
 ical insurance. Using Danish administrative data covering the full populat
 ion over three decades\, together with a novel naming-based measure of ind
 ividual religiosity\, the paper finds that more religious individuals are 
 significantly more likely to be self-employed and to hold a larger share o
 f their assets in stocks. These effects are economically meaningful\, corr
 esponding to roughly a quarter of the wealth effect on economic risk-takin
 g. The findings also remain significant when comparing individuals within 
 sibling groups\, which absorbs family-level confounders. The research furt
 her finds that these same individuals are less likely to be charged with a
  criminal offence. This pattern of opposite effects across different types
  of risk is consistent with the idea that religiosity reduces perceived ba
 ckground risk through a psychological insurance channel\, while simultaneo
 usly increasing the perceived cost of behaviours condemned by religious te
 achings. Overall\, the paper suggests that religiosity helps expla
END:VEVENT
