SUMMARY:[線上] Alain Schläpfer — Why Do Voters Elect Authoritarians? H
 ow Scandal Signals Independence from Elites
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/alain-schlapfer-why-do-voters-elect-a
 uthoritarians-how-scandal-signals-independence-from-elites
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/alain-schlapfer-why-do-voters
 -elect-authoritarians-how-scandal-signals-independence-from-elites\n線上
 連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1\n\nPoliticians who openly display anti-democratic traits can att
 ract substantial electoral support\, even among voters who value democracy
 . This paper argues that the very behavior that makes a politician objecti
 onable can also reassure voters that the politician will remain independen
 t of political elites. Voters whose preferred policies lie far from elite 
 interests worry that the candidates they elect will be captured by elites 
 once in office. Because capturing a politician mired in scandal is costly 
 for elites\, scandalous behavior can credibly signal that a candidate is b
 eyond elite reach. Where elites are democratically committed\, displays of
  anti-democratic intent become an efficient way to generate such a scandal
 . Voters may elect these candidates even though they are more likely to ma
 nipulate elections once in office\, an outcome costly for voters and elite
 s alike. Elites who can perfectly anticipate which politicians will subver
 t elections have no credible way to communicate this to voters. A reanalys
 is of existing experimental data is consistent with the model's central pr
 ediction. Unlike existing accounts of anti-democratic voting\, this theory
  explains why citizens who value democracy may still elect an anti-democra
 tic candidate\, even when a democratically committed candidate offers the 
 same policy platform.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nAlain Schläpfer is a social
  science research scholar at Stanford University’s Center on Democracy\,
  Development and the Rule of Law (CDDRL)\, and a
END:VEVENT
