UID:stanford-54004106866444@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260918T022717Z
DTSTART:20261203T200000Z
DTEND:20261203T211500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Ivetta Sergeeva — The Authoritarian Passport: How States
  Discipline Emigrants Without Repressing Them
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ivetta-sergeeva-the-authoritarian-pas
 sport-how-states-discipline-emigrants-without-repressing-them
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ivetta-sergeeva-the-authorita
 rian-passport-how-states-discipline-emigrants-without-repressing-them\n線
 上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQ
 bWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nCitizenship is conventionally understood as a source of ri
 ghts and protection. It is also a source of dependency: states retain dist
 inctive authority over passports\, legal identities\, civil status\, and m
 obility that citizens need even when they live abroad. Under authoritarian
 ism\, these dependencies become instruments of political control. The stud
 y introduces extraterritorial authoritarian citizenship\, a continuing rel
 ationship in which emigrants depend on origin-state institutions whose pro
 vision of nominally universal rights is politically conditional. It genera
 tes latent leverage: the capacity to impose costs without exercising it ag
 ainst anyone in particular. Emigrants may curb dissent even when never tar
 geted\, and transnational repression activates leverage already at work. U
 sing a four-wave panel survey of 4\,000+ Russian emigrants in over 100 cou
 ntries and 500+ interviews\, the authors show that what emigrants fear is 
 the open-ended legal and administrative reach of the origin state rather t
 han targeted violence\, that this fear suppresses contentious activism spe
 cifically\, and that only the protection of another state reduces it. (In 
 collaboration with Emil Kamalov and Karolina Nugumanova).\n\nABOUT THE SPE
 AKER\n\nIvetta Sergeeva specializes in comparative social science\, focusi
 ng on political behavior\, civil society\, citizenship and migration. In h
 er research\, she employs a mixed-methods approach\, emphasizing surveys\,
  statistical modeling\, experiments\, and interviews. Apar
END:VEVENT
