SUMMARY:[現場] Electric Contrasts Study Evening
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/electric-contrasts-study-evening
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/electric-contrasts-study-even
 ing\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/Kzn8W8MrWvzj3L2y7\n\nElectric Contra
 sts: The Art of the Detail brings together photobooks and magazines to sho
 w how the revolution of photography in the late 19th and early 20th centur
 y changed the history of art. Electric Contrasts takes Kenneth Clark’s l
 andmark 1938 book One Hundred Details from Pictures in the National Galler
 y as a starting point to illuminate the impact black and white photographi
 c details of Old Master paintings had on new ways of seeing in the early 2
 0th century. The Bowes Art and Architecture Library exhibition reveals the
  anachronistic and provocative juxtapositions of artworks in avant-garde m
 agazines and books which created a radically new vision that would go on t
 o influence television and the internet.\n\nThis Study Evening brings toge
 ther Geraldine Johnson and Alexander Nemerov to discuss the role of the de
 tail in art history\, moderated by the exhibition’s curator\, Altair Bra
 ndon-Salmon.\n\n6:00pm: Tour of the Electric Contrasts exhibition in Bowes
  Art and Architecture Library with Altair Brandon-Salmon6:30pm: Introducti
 on to the Study Evening with Altair Brandon-Salmon\, Oshman Hall6:40pm: Ge
 raldine Johnson on Medardo Rosso7:00pm: Alexander Nemerov on André Malrau
 x’s The Voices of Silence7:20pm: Audience Q&AGeraldine Johnson is Associ
 ate Professor in the History of Art at the University of Oxford. She will 
 discuss the Italian sculptor-photographer Medardo Rosso (1858-1928) and hi
 s deployment of details from installation shots taken at the 1904 Salon d'
 Automne in Paris in
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54036622167796@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260923T003808Z
DTSTART:20270521T000000Z
DTEND:20270521T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Kurtis Schaeffer: "Poetry in Tibet: Pushing Boundaries of 
 Buddhist Language and Thought"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/kurtis-schaeffer-poetry-in-tibet-push
 ing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-thought
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/kurtis-schaeffer-poetry-in-ti
 bet-pushing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-thought\n\nAbstract:\n\nTi
 betan writers of kāvya-style poetry exploited the structured flexibility 
 of the medium to praise the heroes and ideals of Buddhist tradition\, to i
 nnovate in both literary form and practices of thought\, and at times even
  to transgress the strictures implicit in other genres of Buddhist literat
 ure. This talk explores the popular genre of single verses intended to ill
 ustrate the poetic features described in Daṇḍin's Mirror of Poetry\, a
 nd suggests that kāvya poetry was an important space in elite literature 
 where pre-modern Tibetan writers could openly push the boundaries of Buddh
 ist language\, thought\, and feeling.\n\n \n\nBio:\n\nKurtis Schaeffer is 
 a student of Buddhist literature in Tibet and the Himalayas. He is the tra
 nslator of The Life of the Buddha (2014) and Buddhist Meditation: Classic 
 Teachings from Tibet (2024)\, both from Penguin Classics\, and the author 
 of Himalayan Hermitess (2004)\, The Culture of the Book in Tibet (2009)\, 
 and other books. He is the Frances Myers Ball Professor in the Department 
 of Religious Studies at the University of Virginia.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54037411908366@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T164734Z
DTSTART:20261119T200000Z
DTEND:20261119T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Jannis Panagiotidis- Racism against East Europeans in Germ
 any: History and Present
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, William J. Perry Conference Room\, Encina Hall Centr
 al\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/racism-against-east-europeans-in-germ
 any-history-and-present
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/racism-against-east-europeans
 -in-germany-history-and-present\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/we
 binar/register/WN_mwn-XbggT_KUsVxkMnQ0xg\n報名/票券: https://luma.com/
 3d1y7ndc\n\nRacism against East Europeans constitutes a major gap in racis
 m research and anti-racism debates. Yet there is a long tradition of treat
 ing the region and its inhabitants as inferior\, with devastating conseque
 nces throughout history. This book is the first comprehensive study of rac
 ism in the entangled histories of Germany and Eastern Europe—a racism th
 at is not based on skin color and affects people despite their mostly phen
 otypical whiteness. Embedded in colonial imaginations of Eastern Europe\, 
 anti-East European racism took its most radical and brutal shape during Na
 zism. But racist knowledge persisted across the rupture of 1945 and the Co
 ld War and took on new shapes after the fall of communism in 1989\, when E
 ast-West migration once again became an important feature of European and 
 German reality. This book makes a case for the long overdue eastward expan
 sion of the contemporary racism debate. \n\n \n\nJannis Panagiotidis is a 
 historian and migration scholar. He is scientific director of the Research
  Center for the History of Transformations (RECET) at the University of Vi
 enna. His publications include the monographs The Unchosen Ones: Diaspora\
 , Nation\, and Migration in Israel and Germany (Indiana UP 2019)\, Postsow
 jetische Migration in Deutschland: eine Einführung (Beltz Juventa 2021)\,
  and Antiosteuropäischer Rassismus in Deutschland: Geschichte und Gegenwa
 rt (Beltz Juventa 2024\, with Hans-Christian Petersen).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54037437866563@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260922T013755Z
DTSTART:20261203T200000Z
DTEND:20261203T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Jana Hunter- The Child of Freedom: Temporal Sovereignty in
  Habsburg Bohemia
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, William J. Perry Conference Room\, Encina Hall Centr
 al\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-child-of-freedom-temporal-soverei
 gnty-in-habsburg-bohemia
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-child-of-freedom-temporal
 -sovereignty-in-habsburg-bohemia\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/w
 ebinar/register/WN_mwn-XbggT_KUsVxkMnQ0xg\n報名/票券: https://luma.com
 /pd3ig2ef\n\nWho decides what time it is? In Habsburg Bohemia\, that quest
 ion drew in nearly every other: nation and empire\, identity and sovereign
 ty\, capital and a newly connected world. Railways and telegraphs created 
 a time shared across distance\, and with it a debate over who had the powe
 r to set it. Histories of time standardization have told a story of unific
 ation\; histories of Habsburg sovereignty\, meanwhile\, have located its c
 onflicts in diets and parliaments. This talk brings them together\, asking
  how an empire tried to synchronize every local clock\, where authority ov
 er time was located\, and why time became a question of sovereignty. After
  1867\, Bohemia's unresolved constitutional status moved into the infrastr
 ucture of daily life: whose observatory produced noon\, which meridian ran
  the trains\, whose clocks kept the time. At stake was not only who ruled 
 Bohemia\, but how time could be controlled at all. \n\nBy the turn of the 
 century\, Bohemians called their divided time dualismus\, the word for the
  constitutional settlement itself. This talk traces these jurisdictional d
 isputes over four decades from railway decrees to universal time. The line
 s of conflict did not run simply between empire and nation: German liberal
 s\, Czech nationalists\, intellectuals\, and businessmen appealed to histo
 ric rights\, national identity\, commercial interest\, and civic embarrass
 ment. Jurisdiction over time was scalar\, dispersed across ministries\, ra
 ilway companies\, international conferences\, and municipal c
END:VEVENT
