SUMMARY:[現場] "Sound and Images of the Invisible World in Japan"
LOCATION:Humanities Center\, Levinthal Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/sound-and-images-of-the-invisible-wor
 ld-in-japan
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/sound-and-images-of-the-invis
 ible-world-in-japan\n\nMultidisciplinary roundtable on perspectives past a
 nd present on yōkai with emphasis on sound and images.\n\nParticipants:\n
 \n   Michael Dylan Foster (University of California\, Davis)\n\n   Fabio R
 ambelli (University of California\, Santa Barbara)\n\n   Fumi Tomita (Univ
 ersity of Massachusetts Amherst)\n\n \n\nBios:\n\nMichael Dylan Foster is 
 Professor in the Department of East Asian Languages and Cultures at the Un
 iversity of California\, Davis. He is the author of Pandemonium and Parade
 : Japanese Monsters and the Culture of Yōkai (University of California Pr
 ess 2009)\, The Book of Yōkai (University of California Press 2015\; 2024
 )\, and numerous articles on folklore\, monsters\, and media. His edited v
 olumes include The Folkloresque: Reframing Folklore in a Popular Culture W
 orld (Utah State University Press 2015)\, Matsuri and Religion: Complexity
 \, Continuity\, and Creativity in Japanese Festivals (Brill 2021) and Japa
 nese Supernatural Tales (Penguin Classics 2026). Since 2022\, he has been 
 the “Yōkai Navigator” for the documentary series YOKAI: Exploring Hid
 den Japanese Folklore (NHK World).\n\nFabio Rambelli is Distinguished Prof
 essor of Japanese Religions and Cultural History and International Shinto 
 Foundation Chair in Shinto Studies at the University of California\, Santa
  Barbara. His many publications include: Buddhas and Kami in Japan (with M
 ark Teeuwen\, 2000)\, Buddhist Materiality (2007)\, The Sea and the Sacred
  in Japan (2018)\, Spirits and Animism in Contemporary Japan (2019)\, Gaga
 ku: The Cul
END:VEVENT
