SUMMARY:[現場] "Yōkai! Songs of the Japanese  Supernatural"
LOCATION:Dinkelspiel Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/yokai-songs-of-the-japanese-supernatu
 ral
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/yokai-songs-of-the-japanese-s
 upernatural\n\nA multimedia presentation of Japanese art and live jazz by 
 the Fumi Tomita Quartet.\n\n  Fumi Tomita\, bass\n  Carl Clements\, woodwi
 nds\n  Jason Ennis\, guitars\n  Gary Fieldman\, drums\n\nFumi Tomita is a 
 jazz bassist\, composer\, educator\, and author.  His newest recording\, Y
 okai! Songs of the Japanese Supernatural will be released in October 2026 
 on Origin records and was inspired by 19th-century woodblock prints of Yok
 ai and other Japanese supernatural creatures derived from folklore.  The a
 lbum will be supported by a Northeast tour during October and November.  H
 is 2019 recording\, The Elephant Vanishes: Jazz Interpretations of the Sho
 rt Stories of Haruki Murakami\, was released to critical acclaim by Origin
  Arts records and was listed in the top ten records of 2019 by Jazziz. His
  2020 release Celebrating Bird: The Music of Charlie Parker was similarly 
 received.   \n\nTomita was active on the New York city jazz scene for over
  fifteen years and has performed at venues all over the U.S. and Canada in
 cluding recent appearances at the International Society of Bassists Confer
 ence in Ann Harbor\, Michigan in June 2023 and the Jazz Education Network 
 conference in New Orleans and a short tour of Georgia\, both in January 20
 24.\n\nAs an author\, his second book\, Introduction to Early Jazz: A Conc
 ise Introduction from Its Beginnings through 1929\, was published by State
  University of New York Press in February 2024 and was a guest on The Buzz
 \, the podcast of the Jazz Journalist Association.  His first 
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54047926358295@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261001T203932Z
DTSTART:20261215T000000Z
DTEND:20261215T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] YoungJu Jo | Data-driven discovery and control of neural c
 omputations
LOCATION:Stanford Neurosciences Building\, James Lin and Nisa Leung Seminar
  Room\, E153
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds-seminar-youngju-jo
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds-seminar-youngju-jo\n\nAb
 stract\n\nThe brain is a nonlinear dynamical system whose evolving populat
 ion activity gives rise to computations that guide behavior. We will prese
 nt a data-driven systems neuroscience framework that integrates large-scal
 e neurophysiology\, interpretable dynamical systems modeling\, and targete
 d perturbations in cognitive animal models. This approach has enabled the 
 discovery of cell-type-specific and brain-wide dynamical structures underl
 ying reward and memory computations\, including attractor manifolds that i
 ntegrate past rewards and steer future decision-making. To enable causal t
 ests and model-guided control of these computations\, we have developed op
 tical and genetic technologies for cellular-resolution\, bidirectional con
 trol of neural populations\, along with optimal control approaches for des
 igning perturbations. Together\, these converging advances set the stage f
 or a closed-loop strategy to measure\, model\, and control neural dynamics
  and computations\, toward the long-term vision of writing complex informa
 tion into the brain.\n\nBio \n\nYoungJu Jo is an Assistant Professor and t
 he Brody Family Faculty Fellow in the Department of Electrical Engineering
  and Computer Sciences and the Helen Wills Neuroscience Institute at the U
 niversity of California\, Berkeley. His laboratory develops and applies ne
 xt-generation neurotechnologies to measure\, model\, and control neural dy
 namics and computations in behaving animals. He completed his postdoctoral
  training in Bioengineering in 2026\, 
END:VEVENT
