SUMMARY:[現場] What Do We Owe Each Other in the Age of AI?
LOCATION:Paul Brest Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/what-do-we-owe-each-other-in-the-age-
 of-ai
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/what-do-we-owe-each-other-in-
 the-age-of-ai\n報名/票券: https://bit.ly/arrow27\n\nPlease note that t
 his event is in-person only\, and RSVPs are requested to attend. Walk-ins 
 are welcome.\n\nRegister now!\n\nThe 2026-27 Arrow Lecture features Betsey
  Stevenson discussing transformative AI\, policy\, and a vision for shared
  societal progress.\n\nArtificial intelligence promises to make us richer.
  But will it make our lives better? At this event\, economist Betsey Steve
 nson will draw on economic history and research on happiness and meaning t
 o demonstrate why greater productivity does not automatically translate in
 to human flourishing. She will explore how we can turn AI’s promise into
  shared progress by pacing job transformation\, returning shares of AI-gen
 erated wealth to the public\, and strengthening sources of meaning beyond 
 paid employment. The goal\, Stevenson will argue\, is not to preserve ever
 y job or reject technological change but to build a society in which our w
 orth as human beings does not depend on outperforming a machine.\n\nSpeake
 rs:\n\n💡 Betsey Stevenson is a professor of economics and public policy
  at the University of Michigan. She is a member of the Board of Directors 
 of Lyft\, Inc and a faculty research associate at the National Bureau of E
 conomic Research\, a fellow of the Ifo Institute for Economic Research in 
 Munich\, and an elected member of the National Academy of Social Insurance
 . Previously\, she served as a member of the Biden-Harris transition team 
 at the U.S. Treasury\, a member of President Obama’s Council of Economic
  Advisers and the Chief Economis
END:VEVENT
