UID:stanford-54098980339839@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T164836Z
DTSTART:20261120T003000Z
DTEND:20261120T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Bones\, Borders\, and Black Mirrors: Can Justice Outlive C
 olonialism?
LOCATION:Alumni Center\, Fisher Conference Center: Lane/Lyons/Lodato
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/bones-borders-and-black-mirrors-can-j
 ustice-outlive-colonialism
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/bones-borders-and-black-mirro
 rs-can-justice-outlive-colonialism\n報名/票券: https://cvent.me/eLOL30
 \n\nJoin IAJS for Bones\, Borders\, and Black Mirrors: Can Justice Outlive
  Colonialism? as we consider how colonialism continues to shape our world 
 and explore where\, in the law\, in the archaeological record\, and in art
 \, we are finding ways to build more just societies.\n\nInspiring this con
 versation is artwork by Pedro Lasch\, Coatlicue & Las Meninas: The Stanfor
 d Edition\, originally commissioned as part of a national program co-hoste
 d by Stanford’s Institute for Advancing Just Societies and Zócalo Publi
 c Square\, and now installed at the Institute. In conversation with artist
  and professor\, Pedro Lasch (Duke University)\, are international legal s
 cholar Tendayi Achiume (Stanford Law School)\, and archeology professor Kr
 ish Seetah (Doerr School of Sustainability)\, moderated by sociologist and
  IAJS Faculty Director\, Tomás Jiménez (Sociology).\n\nIdeas Mosaic is a
 n Institute for Advancing Just Societies quarterly series where scholars\,
  practitioners\, and artists converge to shape bold ideas for a more just 
 world.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54099709560880@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260929T182510Z
DTSTART:20270205T000000Z
DTEND:20270205T043000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Shosan Victoria Austin: "Zen Work Practice: Cultivating a 
 Field of Blessings"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/zen-work-practice-cultivating-a-field
 -of-blessings
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/zen-work-practice-cultivating
 -a-field-of-blessings\n\nWhat can ancient work practices offer Silicon Val
 ley today? Hidden in more than a thousand years of Sōtō Zen monastic cod
 es\, we can trace the development of what we might now call a 90-day serio
 us role-playing game. Its purpose: to spiritually mature practitioners\, s
 eparately and together\, for their own benefit and that of all beings.\n\n
 In this lecture\, we will explore aspects of Chinese\, Japanese\, and West
 ern monastic work codes to find out how they promote human-hearted qualiti
 es such as generosity\, morality\, forbearance\, and energy. Familiarity w
 ith these practices can help us illuminate the meaning and function of our
  responsibilities to ourselves\, our work groups\, our communities\, and o
 ur world.\n\n4:00pm - Guided Meditation (Room TBD)\n\n7:00pm - Lecture (Ea
 st Asia Library\, Room 224)\n\nBio:\n\nShosan Victoria Austin began practi
 cing Zen in 1971. She trained in the U.S.\, in India\, and in Japan\, and 
 is entrusted as a Dharma heir in the lineage of Shunryu Suzuki Roshi (1999
 )\, an international Soto Zen priest (2005)\, and a Dharma teacher at San 
 Francisco Zen Center (1999). Some of her previous San Francisco Zen Center
  roles include Work Leader\, Tenzo\, Tanto\, and President. In 2022\, she 
 co-led the training and participation of the Ryoban (ceremonial assembly) 
 for the Zenshuji 100th Anniversary Jukai-e in Los Angeles\, a five-day ret
 reat in which over 100 people received precept teachings and vows.\n\nIn a
 ddition to her deep study and daily teaching of yogic aspects of meditatio
 n—in div
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54110219615265@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261007T192850Z
DTSTART:20261112T193000Z
DTEND:20261113T010000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Unsettling the New AI Status Quo
LOCATION:Tresidder Union\, Cypress Lounge\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/unsettling-the-new-ai-status-quo
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/unsettling-the-new-ai-status-
 quo\n報名/票券: https://ethicsinsociety.stanford.edu/events/unsettling
 -new-ai-status-quo\n\nJoin this thought-provoking showcase on the practica
 l implementation of ethics developed by the 2026 Ethics and Technology Pra
 ctitioner Fellows.\n\nThe Ethics and Technology Practitioner Fellows have 
 dedicated the past year to exploring and addressing critical issues at the
  intersection of technology and ethics through inquiry\, stakeholder engag
 ement\, and pushing for reforms in organizations and fields of practice. P
 rojects are in areas ranging from community deliberation on data centers\,
  to AI-accelerated carbon emissions\, to regulating AI slop. We welcome an
 yone invested in the future of technology\, including those in civil socie
 ty\, policymaking\, industry\, and academia to come join the discussion of
  how we exercise leadership in a space filled with barriers and complexiti
 es working against change.\n\nSee the full showcase schedule.\n\nThis even
 t will have a photographer present to document the event. No personal reco
 rdings (audio or visual) are allowed. By attending\, you consent for your 
 image to be used for Stanford-related promotions and materials. If you hav
 e any questions\, please contact ethics-center@stanford.edu.\n\nIf you req
 uire disability-related accommodation\, please contact disability.access@s
 tanford.edu as soon as possible or at least 7 business days in advance of 
 the event.\n\nSee more information about visitor parking. \n\nLearn more a
 bout the McCoy Family Center for Ethics in Society.
