BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54112511034966@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261019T171607Z
DTSTART:20261113T003000Z
DTEND:20261113T020000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Making Letters: Typography Across Hand\, Screen\, and Loom
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/making-letters-typography-across-hand
 -screen-and-loom
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/making-letters-typography-acr
 oss-hand-screen-and-loom\n\nRSVP Link: HTTPS://TINYURL.COM/STSAMY26\n\nHow
  do the tools used to create letterforms shape the way those forms are ima
 gined\, constructed\, and understood? In this presentation\, graphic desig
 ner\, educator\, type designer\, and fiber artist Amy Copeland traces her 
 evolving relationship with typography across analog\, digital\, and textil
 e practices. Beginning with hand-lettered high school book covers and the 
 construction of letterforms with gouache and a ruling pen in college\, she
  follows this trajectory through professional art direction and digital ty
 pesetting\, original typeface design\, and her recent project of weaving t
 he alphabet.\n\nThrough this practice-based account\, Copeland considers h
 ow typographic knowledge moves between technologies and materials. Digital
  tools make letterforms reproducible\, scalable\, and precisely adjustable
 \; weaving translates those same forms into physical structures governed b
 y warp\, weft\, tension\, sequence\, and touch. These material constraints
  do more than alter the appearance of a letter—they influence how it is 
 conceived and constructed.Rather than treating analog and digital processe
 s as opposites\, Copeland explores the productive exchange between them. H
 er work demonstrates how knowledge gained through one form of making can m
 igrate into another\, revealing letters not as fixed symbols but as adapta
 ble structures shaped by tools\, materials\, and embodied experience.\n\n-
 ---------\n\nAmy Copeland loves to make things. A graphic designer and edu
 cator f
END:VEVENT
