SUMMARY:[現場] The Greek and Arabic Reception of Ptolemy's Almagest betwe
 en the 7th and 9th Centuries | Maria Mavroudi
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ptolemys-almagest
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ptolemys-almagest\n報名/票
 券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSffd2KijnqGN3E334Z5Fqh8TiOsY
 LtGjmXtfssGV8jcerexbQ/viewform?usp=header\n\nThe period between the sevent
 h and the ninth centuries has frequently been portrayed in scholarship as 
 “dark”\, especially because its intellectual production appears low. O
 verall\, it is deemed as an empty interval between the end of the intellec
 tual efflorescence of antiquity and the presumed renaissance of philosophy
  and the sciences in the Arab world enabled by the Greek-to-Arabic transla
 tion movement in ninth- and tenth-century Baghdad. This talk will re-exami
 ne these scholarly assessments by using the reception of Ptolemy’s Almag
 est as an example.\n\n\n\nMaria Mavroudi was born in Thessaloniki\, Greece
  and studied Philology at the University of her native city before earning
  a Ph.D. in Byzantine studies at Harvard. Her scholarly work begun by focu
 sing on a tenth-century Byzantine book on dream interpretation that had be
 en widely received in Latin and the European vernaculars and counted as th
 e Christian dreambook of the Middle Ages. While generally viewed as a Byza
 ntine invention partly based on the second-century manual of Artemidorus\,
  she showed that it was a Christian adaptation of Arabic Islamic material 
 and one among a larger group of texts originally written in Arabic or Pers
 ian and received into Greek between the ninth and the fifteenth centuries.
  During the next two decades\, she worked on identifying the place of thes
 e translations within Byzantine literary culture and its reception in “E
 ast” and “West’ during the medieval and early modern period. This be
 gs reconsideri
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54125978094433@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T122432Z
DTSTART:20261103T233000Z
DTEND:20261104T004500Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Book Talk — We Shall Outlast Them: Putin's Global Campai
 gn to Defeat the West
LOCATION:Encina Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-talk-we-shall-outlast-them-putin
 s-global-campaign-to-defeat-the-west
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-talk-we-shall-outlast-th
 em-putins-global-campaign-to-defeat-the-west\n線上連結: https://stanfo
 rd.zoom.us/j/93963090864?pwd=i2uxiraWsDRoO7K3iYQZMkZFxalZmM.1\n報名/票
 券: https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/book-talk-we-shall-outlast-them
 -putins-global-campaign-defeat-west\n\nHow has Russia sustained its confro
 ntation with the West since 2022\, and at what cost? In her new book\, Han
 na Notte argues the Kremlin is waging a war not only on Ukraine but over t
 he global order itself.\n\nNotte traces how Russia adapted after the invas
 ion. It worked around sanctions\, built new partnerships\, cultivated supp
 ort across the Global South and tried to deepen divisions within Western d
 emocracies. Her account combines policy analysis with field reporting from
  more than a dozen countries and interviews with officials\, facilitators 
 and ordinary people drawn into Russia's agenda.\n\nThe book also examines 
 the domestic consequences. Notte finds that Putin's focus on confronting t
 he West has distorted Russia's economy and moved the country from a tacit 
 dictatorship toward a full one. She suggests Putin may outlast the West\, 
 but has gambled Russia's future in the process.\n\nFSI Satre Family Senior
  Fellow and Mosbacher Director of CDDRL\, Kathryn Stoner will moderate the
  discussion\, which will cover Russian statecraft\, the durability of auto
 cratic regimes under pressure\, and what Russia's strategy means for the U
 .S.\, Europe\, and the Global South. Audience Q&A will follow.\n\nABOUT TH
 E SPEAKER\n\nHanna Notte is the Director of the Eurasia Nonproliferation P
 rogram at the James Martin Center for Nonproliferation Studies. She is als
 o a senior associate (non-resident) with the Center for Strategic and Inte
 rnational Studies and with the Kennan Institute. Notte's expertise is in R
 ussia's
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54126220446841@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T122433Z
DTSTART:20261029T010000Z
DTEND:20261029T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Film Screening - American Solitaire
LOCATION:Li Ka Shing Center\, 130
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/film-screening-american-solitaire
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/film-screening-american-solit
 aire\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSebtazSGQI_6
 k9kBhUfMCopppTI9-pXw0WpFwH9qL-nvI_dbw/viewform\n\nReclaiming Healing and R
 esponsibility\nWhat does it mean to return home from war\, and who helps c
 arry the weight when you do?\n\nAmerican Solitaire is a moving new narrati
 ve film from writer-director Aaron Davidman\, executive produced by David 
 Oyelowo\, that follows a veteran grappling with the psychological\, moral\
 , and cultural aftermath of service. It’s a story about isolation\, acco
 untability\, and finding connection in a divided country.\n\nThrough scree
 nings and community conversations\, the film team & Picture Motion invite 
 audiences to use the film as a springboard for dialogue on veterans’ men
 tal health\, moral injury\, and collective healing. Community discussion w
 ith film director Aaron Davidman\, IAVA President Dr. Kyleanne Hunter and 
 Stanford School of Medicine’s Dr. Dean Winslow\, retired Lt. Colonel.
END:VEVENT
