SUMMARY:[線上] Shop Smart\, Eat Well: Budget-Friendly Grocery Tips\, Meal
  Ideas and Label-Reading Strategies
LOCATION:https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-events.html#shop-smart
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/shop-smart-eat-well-budget-friendly-g
 rocery-tips-meal-ideas-and-label-reading-strategies
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/shop-smart-eat-well-budget-fr
 iendly-grocery-tips-meal-ideas-and-label-reading-strategies\n線上連結:
  https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-events.html#shop-smart\n報
 名/票券: https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-events.html#shop-s
 mart\n\nErin Hodgson\, MS\, RD\, BC-ADM\, CDCES\, offers practical strateg
 ies to stretch a grocery budget while building diabetes-friendly meals. Sh
 e will share how to read nutrition labels to compare nutrition information
 \, identify added sugars and hidden sodium\, and plan cost-effective meal 
 ideas that support steady glycemic control. \n\nThis presentation is part 
 of the monthly Diabetes Wellness Group webinar series from the Stanford Me
 dicine Diabetes Care Program. Webinars are free and open to adults with di
 abetes and their families. You don’t need to be a Stanford patient to jo
 in.\n\nThis webinar will be presented by Erin Hodgson\, MS\, RD\, BC-ADM\,
  CDCES\, who is a clinical dietitian and certified diabetes care and educa
 tion specialist at Stanford Health Care.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54136388473483@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T122505Z
DTSTART:20261030T190000Z
DTEND:20261030T210000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Gatekeepers: Hamburg\, Germany and the Making of the M
 odern World
LOCATION:Building 200\, History Corner\, 307
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-gatekeepers-hamburg-germany-and-t
 he-making-of-the-modern-world
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-gatekeepers-hamburg-germa
 ny-and-the-making-of-the-modern-world\n\nThe Gatekeepers is a history of t
 he relationship between liberalism and globalization told through Hamburg\
 , the city known as Germany’s “gateway to the world.” It tells the s
 tory of this city’s upper stratum\, a wide-ranging but remarkably durabl
 e network of people and institutions centered on Hamburg but spiraling out
  into the world. For one century\, this network committed itself to pullin
 g Germany into the world economy and to pulling the promises of an interco
 nnected world into Germany—yet also to wielding the power to determine w
 hat it meant to be open to the world. As they did so\, Hamburg’s gatekee
 pers laid key foundations for the postwar Federal Republic of Germany’s 
 foreign affairs and the architecture of post-1945 world order.\n\nJoin the
  Modern Europe Workshop in learning more about and discussing Jack Guenthe
 r's upcoming work. RSVP here for lunch.\n\nJack H. Guenther received his P
 h.D. in history from Princeton in 2024. He specializes in German history a
 nd the history of capitalism\, liberalism\, and international order. His f
 irst book\, The Gatekeepers: Hamburg\, Germany\, and the Making of the Mod
 ern World\, is under contract with Yale University Press. His other resear
 ch and writing have appeared in Archiv für Sozialgeschichte\, Central Eur
 opean History\, The English Historical Review\, German History\, H-Diplo\,
  História Econômica & História de Empresas\, and in edited volumes on G
 ermany’s export economy and contemporary European foreign affairs.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54136475283456@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261019T215441Z
DTSTART:20261111T003000Z
DTEND:20261111T020000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] A Rift in Time. Reinhart Koselleck and the Theory of Histo
 ry
LOCATION:Building 200\, History Corner\, 307
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/a-rift-in-time-reinhart-koselleck-and
 -the-theory-of-history
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/a-rift-in-time-reinhart-kosel
 leck-and-the-theory-of-history\n\nWhat possible futures does history conta
 in? Reinhart Koselleck has become one the most widely read and cited twent
 ieth-century German historians and theorists of history\, not just for the
  new field of “global conceptual history” but essentially for anyone a
 cross disciplines writing about questions of time\, temporality\, and hist
 orical thought. Yet the recent uptick of interest in his work as part of t
 he “temporal turn” obscures the fact that Koselleck was working throug
 hout his life on something even more ambitious: a new theory of history or
  rather a theory of the conditions of possible histories\, and hence possi
 ble futures\, written by an historian in an age of catastrophes.\n\nBased 
 on a wealth of previously inaccessible archival materials\, this talk expl
 ores Koselleck’s critique of the modern concept of History and what he c
 alled its “temporalization”—from Critique and Crisis to his essays o
 n dreams and war memorials—and parses out his parallel search for an ant
 idote in the form of a new theory of historical knowledge\, most visible i
 n his late essay collection Sediments of Time. We will ask what remains of
  Koselleck’s theoretical reckoning with ruptured time and repetition in 
 history and whether it can be untangled from the experiences of loss and c
 omplicity of his generation.\n\nJoin the Modern Europe Workshop in learnin
 g more about and discussing Stefan-Ludgwig Hoffman's research. RSVP for re
 freshments.\n\nStefan-Ludwig Hoffmann is Chancellor’s Professor of Histo
 ry at the Unive
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54161263754671@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261005T181541Z
DTSTART:20261113T000000Z
DTEND:20261113T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Delivering Humanitarian Aid in a Contested World
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, Bechtel Conference Center
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/delivering-humanitarian-aid-in-a-cont
 ested-world
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/delivering-humanitarian-aid-i
 n-a-contested-world\n報名/票券: https://app.smartsheet.com/b/form/01a0
 872afab87e4fb63479be86ac5f29\n\nJoin us for a two-part panel discussion wi
 th IsraAID and partners exploring the future of humanitarian assistance in
  an increasingly contested and conflict-ridden world. The first panel will
  examine how aid organizations deliver relief when access\, neutrality\, a
 nd safety are absent or precarious\, drawing on IsraAID's experience respo
 nding to crises across the globe. The second panel will turn to the Middle
  East\, focusing on the promise and practical challenges of Arab-Israeli c
 ooperation in delivering humanitarian assistance to communities in need ar
 ound the region.\n\nAGENDA\n3:30 - 4:00 PM: GATHERING AND REGISTRATION\n\n
 4:00 - 4:10 PM: INTRODUCTION\n\nSpeaker: Larry Diamond (Mosbacher Senior F
 ellow in Global Democracy\, Freeman Spogli Institute for International Stu
 dies\, Stanford University)\n\n4:10 - 4:50 PM: PANEL ONE — Delivering Hu
 manitarian Assistance in 2026: Challenges and Opportunities\n\nSonali Kord
 e (Senior Advisor\, All Hands and Hearts)Raphael Marcus (Deputy CEO\, Isra
 AID)Moderator: David Cornfield (Anne T. and Robert M. Bass Professor of Pe
 diatric Pulmonary Medicine\, Stanford University)\n\n4:55 - 6:00 PM: PANEL
  TWO — Lessons from the Middle East: Arab-Israeli Cooperation in Humanit
 arian Aid\n\nDr. Yaroup Ajlouni (President\, Jordan Health Aid Society Int
 ernational)Diana Shehade-Nama (Director of Fundraising\, Marketing and Com
 munications\, Rozana–Health Diplomacy)Yotam Polizer (Global CEO\, IsraAI
 D)Dr. Khaula Sawah (President\, Union of Medical Care and Relief Organizat
 ions-USA)Mode
END:VEVENT
