SUMMARY:[現場] Big Tech\, Democracy\, and Human Rights
LOCATION:Alumni Center\, Fisher Conference Room ( Barnes/McDowell/Cranston)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/big-tech-democracy-and-human-rights
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/big-tech-democracy-and-human-
 rights\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/uGhKMyy1MWLvuJqu9\n\nThis confere
 nce is hosted by the Center for South Asia (Stanford Global Studies)\, wit
 h the Center for the Study of Organized Hate (CSOH)\, We Are Noor\, and DR
 F USA.\n\nAbout the conference\nA handful of technology companies now shap
 e how billions of people communicate and take part in public life\, and mo
 st of those people live in the Global Majority: Africa\, Latin America\, t
 he Caribbean\, and parts of Asia and Oceania. The day asks why rights and 
 safety protections stop at the borders of a few rich countries\, and what 
 it would take for them to reach the people who need them most. Three panel
 s and four conversations move from ideology (the tech right)\, to geopolit
 ics (AI governance)\, to remedies (online harms and accountability)\, to t
 he labor and money that sustain counter-power.\n\nPanels and Firesides\nPa
 nel 1: The Tech Right and the Global Majority : Ideology\, Design\, and th
 e Politics of Inevitability\nFireside: What Silicon Valley's Authoritarian
  Embrace Means for the Global Majority\nPanel 2: AI Governance and the Geo
 politics of Emerging Technology\nPanel 3: Online Harms and Accountability:
  Why protection Stops at the Borders\nFireside: Who Pays for Accountabilit
 y? Funding Digital Rights After the Cuts\n\nMore details about the Big Tec
 h\, Democracy\, and Human Rights Conference can be found here.
END:VEVENT
