SUMMARY:[現場] French Speaking Worlds: Audrey Celestine - Negritude Exper
 iments: Aimé Césaire and the Politics of Culture in Martinique (1970s)
LOCATION:Building 260\, Pigott Hall\, Rm 252
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/french-speaking-worlds-audrey-celesti
 ne-negritude-experiments-aime-cesaire-and-the-politics-of-culture-in-marti
 nique-1970s
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/french-speaking-worlds-audrey
 -celestine-negritude-experiments-aime-cesaire-and-the-politics-of-culture-
 in-martinique-1970s\n\nPlease join French Speaking Worlds: Then and Now fo
 r Negritude Experiments: Aimé Césaire and the Politics of Culture in Mar
 tinique (1970s)\, a talk with Audrey Celestine (New York University)\n\nAb
 stract: How can processes of emancipation be conceived within a political 
 framework that does not entail independence? What forms can decolonization
  take in a society that remains institutionally embedded within a former c
 olonial power? And how did culture come to occupy a central place in both 
 political thought and practice in this context? More specifically\, what r
 ole did Aimé Césaire\, as both intellectual and elected official\, play 
 in articulating and implementing such a project? In this paper\, I use Lé
 opold Sédar Senghor’s visit to Martinique in February 1976 as a pivotal
  event through which to examine the political and social dynamics at the h
 eart of the study of Aimé Césaire’s cultural policy during his tenure 
 as mayor of Fort--de-France. Initially conceived as a case study within a 
 broader section on Césaire’s diasporic ambitions\, the visit instead em
 erges as a structuring thread\, bringing into focus the tensions between l
 ocal\, national\, and diasporic political imaginaries and practices. The e
 ncounter between Senghor and Césaire thus offers a lens through which to 
 explore the possibilities and contradictions of an emancipatory project pu
 rsued within\, rather than outside\, the French political framework—and 
 the central role culture came to play in defining its horizons.\n\nAudrey 
 Cé
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54177973515463@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261020T221759Z
DTSTART:20261106T013000Z
DTEND:20261106T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Queer Thing about Sin | Harry Tanner
LOCATION:Humanities Center\, Levinthal Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-queer-thing-about-sin-harry-tanne
 r
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-queer-thing-about-sin-har
 ry-tanner\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeqJgPk
 Bi9yPAjkLWSQtvv2htLXjua34iEHDDVcdTvseuBqCA/viewform\n\nIn The Queer Thing 
 About Sin (Bloomsbury 2025)\, Harry Tanner reveals the ancient origins of 
 homophobia. By the end of the 5th century BCE\, Greek plays were being sta
 ged which ridiculed homosexuality\, by the end of the next century Athenia
 ns were being tried for homosexuality and Plato had explicitly condemned i
 t in his Laws. In Rome\, the legal situation was hardly better and in the 
 law codes of Leviticus same-sex sex is condemned. Why did this come to hap
 pen\, and what warnings does it hold for us now? \n\nTanner will explore s
 ome of his findings in this talk and then turn his attention to how to wri
 te and research academic research for a trade audience — he will argue t
 hat trade publishers have unfairly received a reputation for simplifying r
 esearch\, and his method for going from a PhD to a book proposal and an in
 ternationally published trade book. His talk will address the need to adap
 t scholarly writing and research and work with Artificial Intelligence to 
 build new\, more human insights. He will also talk briefly about his own e
 xperience of conversion therapy and how it shaped his research in Classics
 . \n\nHarry Tanner earned a PhD in Classics at the University of Galway\, 
 Ireland (2023). He has published on Greek Linguistics\, specifically on se
 mantic change in Ancient Greek\, appeared on SiriusXM and a variety of pod
 casts including The Guilty Feminist\, and written for The New York Sun. Hi
 s book The Queer Thing About Sin was shortlisted for Book of the Year in t
 he UK by Foyles. H
END:VEVENT
