BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54178976599441@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261007T180235Z
DTSTART:20261201T233000Z
DTEND:20261202T003000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Applied Physics/Physics Colloquium: Jo Dunkley - "The Simo
 ns Observatory"
LOCATION:Hewlett Teaching Center\, 201
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/applied-physicsphysics-colloquium-jo-
 dunkley-the-simons-observatory
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/applied-physicsphysics-colloq
 uium-jo-dunkley-the-simons-observatory\n\nAbstract: The Simons Observatory
 \, a new millimeter-wave observatory in the Atacama\, is now gathering dat
 a. With its smaller telescopes we are seeking a primordial signal imprinte
 d by gravitational waves generated in the early universe. Its largest tele
 scope is measuring the finer scales of the CMB with increased sensitivity\
 , probing the initial conditions of the early universe as well as revealin
 g the later-formed cosmic web of gas and dark matter through the gravitati
 onal lensing and Sunyaev Zel’dovich effects. It will map half the sky at
  arcminute resolution\, overlapping with a number of large-scale structure
  surveys including Rubin. By surveying half the sky every couple of days\,
  we also hope to see new types of transient astronomical events in millime
 ter-wavelengths.  I will talk about the current status of SO\, and its sci
 ence plans.\n\nJo Dunkley is the Joseph Henry Professor of Physics and Ast
 rophysical Sciences at Princeton University. She works on the analysis and
  interpretation of cosmological observations. She is currently the Spokesp
 erson for the Simons Observatory collaboration. Previously she was a membe
 r of NASA's WMAP science team and a scientist on the European Space Agency
 ’s Planck satellite project\, and was the analysis leader for the Atacam
 a Cosmology Telescope. She is a Fellow of the Royal Society.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54179547167136@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T122858Z
DTSTART:20261031T003000Z
DTEND:20261031T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Film Screening of the 2026 Lithuanian submission for the A
 cademy Awards – How to Divorce During the War
LOCATION:Bishop Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/film-screening-how-to-divorce-during-
 the-war
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/film-screening-how-to-divorce
 -during-the-war\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/8RAxbW8n3qscPdSe9\n\nJoi
 n us for a film screening of Lithuania's submission for the Academy Awards
  with commentary from Maarja Merivoo-Parro\, CEO of Vabamu Museum of Occup
 ations and Freedom.\n\nHow to Divorce During the War takes place in Vilniu
 s\, Lithuania in 2022. High-flying executive\, Marija (Zygimante Elena Jak
 staite)\, chooses the worst possible moment to inform her husband\, Vytas 
 (Marius Repsys)\, that she wants a divorce: just one day before Russia’s
  invasion of Ukraine. Suddenly the couple must navigate the process of sep
 aration as it collides with a refugee and mid-life crisis. A struggling fi
 lm director\, Vytas is put into a tight spot by having to move in with his
  Russophile parents\, while Marija struggles to find her footing as the wa
 r affects both her business and home life. \n\nAs the reason for the divor
 ce gradually becomes clear\, the dissolution of Marija and Vytas’ marria
 ge creates conflicts and opportunities for both of them\, while also affec
 ting their ever-observant young daughter\, Dovile. Packed with surprises a
 long with much heartache and soul searching\, Andrius Blaževičius’ nua
 nced and timely family drama skillfully depicts the fallout of a marriage 
 against the backdrop of extraordinary events with humor and compassion. \n
 \nDr. Maarja Merivoo-Parro is the CEO of Vabamu Museum of Occupations and 
 Freedom in Tallinn\, Estonia. She is a public intellectual dedicated to ex
 ploring the history of mentality at the crossroads of culture and politics
 . She is a Fulbright scholar and Marie Curie
END:VEVENT
