 z27e4Qxkkmd82zDENq7FQwMA9D0mr84qZPYexRoltxeJe6tQ&_hsmi=2\n\nThis event wil
 l pay tribute to Milorad M. Drašković (Drachkovitch)\, who devoted more 
 than three decades of his career to the Hoover Institution\, on the thirti
 eth anniversary of his passing. From 1974 to 1984\, Drašković led the Ho
 over Institution Library and Archives\, helping to shape its vast collecti
 ons and to support visiting researchers from around the world. He strength
 ened the ties between the Serbian\, Yugoslav\, and broader communities int
 erested in the meaning of our shared history.\n\nAttendees will also enjoy
  exclusive access to the newly reopened Polich Library (formerly the Polic
 h Room)\, named in honor of Todor and Andja Polich. From 1968 to 2020\, th
 e room hosted seminars and meetings with distinguished scholars and intern
 ational visitors at the Hoover Institution. Today\, it carries that legacy
  forward as a space devoted to the study of the history of Yugoslavia.\n\n
 ABOUT THE SPEAKERS\nDennis Bark is an emeritus senior fellow at the Hoover
  Institution.\n\nHe is the author or editor of nine books\, including Refl
 ections on Europe (1997)\, an edited volume to which he contributed the es
 say “The American-European Relationship: Reflections on Half a Century\,
  1947–1997.” His most recent work is Europeans and Americans Dancing i
 n the Dark (2007). In March 1997\, he was awarded the Knight’s Cross of 
 the Order of Merit\, First Class\, by the Federal Republic of Germany in r
 ecognition of his contributions to German-American relations. In October 2
 001\, he received the Knight’s Cross of t
END:VEVENT
