SUMMARY:[混合] Book Talk: Chrystia Freeland — Unreliable Boyfriend: An 
 Insider's View of Dealing with a Chaotic Superpower\, Plutocrats\, and Oth
 er Complicated People
LOCATION:Encina Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-talk-chrystia-freeland-unreliabl
 e-boyfriend-an-insiders-view-of-dealing-with-a-chaotic-superpower-plutocra
 ts-and-other-complicated-people
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-talk-chrystia-freeland-u
 nreliable-boyfriend-an-insiders-view-of-dealing-with-a-chaotic-superpower-
 plutocrats-and-other-complicated-people\n線上連結: https://stanford.zo
 om.us/j/96625253979?pwd=KqJdckdVxlr3gHBjPayBLbbpyVFZQS.1\n線上資訊:\nP
 assword if prompted: 123456\n報名/票券: https://app.smartsheet.com/b/f
 orm/01a11c60b90c7da7a88e2c34197ffce5\n\nHow should allies deal with a supe
 rpower they can no longer rely on? Former Canadian Deputy Prime Minister C
 hrystia Freeland joins CDDRL to discuss her new book\, "Unreliable Boyfrie
 nd: An Insider's View of Dealing with a Chaotic Superpower\, Plutocrats\, 
 and Other Complicated People."\n\nFreeland has seen these questions from t
 wo sides. As a foreign correspondent\, she covered the rise of Russia's ol
 igarchs in the 1990s. As a senior Canadian official\, she helped renegotia
 te the trade agreement between Canada\, the U.S. and Mexico. The book draw
 s on both careers to examine how power operates inside government\, why tr
 ade\, security and democracy can no longer be treated separately\, and how
  democracies might build coalitions strong enough to resist authoritarian 
 states and domestic oligarchs.\n\nFreeman Spogli Institute (FSI) Satre Fam
 ily Senior Fellow and Mosbacher Director of CDDRL\, Kathryn Stoner will mo
 derate the conversation\, focusing on democratic resilience\, internationa
 l political economy\, U.S. foreign policy\, Russia and Ukraine.  Audience 
 Q&A will follow. Books will be available for purchase at the event.  \n\nA
 BOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nChrystia Freeland is Canada’s former Deputy Prime Mi
 nister\, Foreign Minister\, and Minister of Finance. With a background in 
 Russian history\, she first gained international acclaim as a foreign corr
 espondent and editor for the Financial Times and Reuters\, and later serve
 d as the deputy editor of The Globe and Mail. Her first book\, Sale of the
  Century\, told 
END:VEVENT
