UID:stanford-54189776217283@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T195600Z
DTSTART:20261028T020000Z
DTEND:20261028T030000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Public Lecture: Gravitational Wave Detection
LOCATION:Sapp Center for Science Teaching and Learning\, 111
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/public-lecture-gravitational-wave-det
 ection
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/public-lecture-gravitational-
 wave-detection\n線上資訊:\nThis lecture will also be livestreamed on Y
 ouTube.  Live stream URL can be found at the bottom of the EventBrite reg
 istration confirmation email.\n報名/票券: https://www.eventbrite.com/e
 /public-lecture-gravitational-wave-detection-registration-2003171678414\n\
 nHow do we detect tiny stretches of spacetime? Join us to learn how sensit
 ive instruments are built to enable scientific discoveries!\n\nThis lectur
 e will be offered in a hybrid format\, is open to all\, and is recommended
  for adults and students in 9th grade and above.\n\nTitle: Measuring the I
 mpossible: How We Detected Gravitational Waves\n\nSpeaker: Dr. Alexandra M
 itchell (Stanford/KIPAC)\n\nAbstract: Two black holes collide over a billi
 on light-years away\, creating waves in the fabric of spacetime. But by th
 e time those waves reach us\, their effect is so small that they would cha
 nge the light-years of distance by less than the width of a human hair. Wh
 en Einstein predicted gravitational waves as a consequence of his theory o
 f general relativity\, detecting them seemed far beyond what experiments c
 ould achieve. Doing so would require an instrument more sensitive than any
 thing ever built before. At this extraordinary level of precision\, almost
  everything gets in the way: ground motion\, the thermal vibrations of ato
 ms making up the detector\, even the quantum nature of light. In this talk
 \, Dr. Alexandra Mitchell will explore how generations of scientists and e
 ngineers turned a measurement that once seemed impossible into one of the 
 great experimental achievements of modern physics. She will also highlight
  some of LIGO’s recent gravitational-wave discoveries and what they have
  revealed about our Universe\, and how researchers are pushing these instr
 uments even further to discover what we h
END:VEVENT
