SUMMARY:[現場] “Don’t Eat the Mermaid: Exploring the Legend of Yaobik
 uni”
LOCATION:Humanities Center\, Levinthal Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/dont-eat-the-mermaid-exploring-the-le
 gend-of-yaobikuni
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/dont-eat-the-mermaid-explorin
 g-the-legend-of-yaobikuni\n\nScreening of a recent documentary on Yaobikun
 i followed by a conversation with Michael Dylan Foster and Hank Glassman\n
 \nDescription:\n\nThe legend of Yaobikuni tells of a young woman who inadv
 ertently eats the flesh of a ningyo (mermaid) and is therefore blessed—o
 r cursed—with immortality and eternal youth. Outliving her family and fr
 iends\, she eventually abandons society to travel throughout Japan as a nu
 n. Finally\, after eight hundred years she returns to her birthplace\, cur
 rent day Obama City in Fukui Prefecture\, to enter into eternal prayer in 
 a local cave. \n\nFor this presentation we will first screen a recent tele
 vision documentary (28 mins) that explores how Yaobikuni is understood by 
 current residents in Obama. This will be followed by a conversation about 
 the legend itself\, the broader Buddhist context surrounding it\, and the 
 making of the documentary. \n\nParticipants: \n\n  Michael Dylan Foster (U
 niversity of California\, Davis) \n\n  Hank Glassman (Haverford College) \
 n\n \n\nBios: \n\nMichael Dylan Foster is Professor in the Department of E
 ast Asian Languages and Cultures at the University of California\, Davis. 
 He is the author of Pandemonium and Parade: Japanese Monsters and the Cult
 ure of Yōkai (University of California Press 2009)\, The Book of Yōkai (
 University of California Press 2015\; 2024)\, and numerous articles on fol
 klore\, monsters\, and media. His edited volumes include The Folkloresque:
  Reframing Folklore in a Popular Culture World (Utah State University Pres
 s 2015)\, Mat
END:VEVENT
