SUMMARY:[現場] SCRIPTA - Jutta Eming: A Sleeping Giant: The Transmission 
 of the Sindbad / Seven Sages of Rome-Narrative
LOCATION:Bldg. 200\, Rm. 307
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/scripta-jutta-eming-a-sleeping-giant
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/scripta-jutta-eming-a-sleepin
 g-giant\n\nThe Sindbad / Seven Sages of Rome-material is the most famous p
 remodern narrative tradition of which nobody has ever heard. Told in at le
 ast 30 languages from Persia to Iceland over more than 10 centuries\, the 
 story cycle recounts a medieval #me-too story of a false rape accusation b
 etween a mute prince and his young stepmother at a royal court\, and the i
 mpossibility of establishing the truth. As such\, it is one of the most po
 pular works of medieval and early modern literature. The modest amount of 
 scholarly attention that the tradition has received remains fragmentary\, 
 and almost exclusively linked to modern national languages. This also mean
 s that the unique insights into the cultural diversity and intercultural c
 ommunication embedded in this narrative tradition have been mostly overloo
 ked. To meet this deficiency\, recent collaborative research projects whic
 h the speaker takes part in argue for a renewed comparative philological\,
  literary\, and cultural analysis. In this context\, a data base has been 
 created that for the first time contains all known textual witnesses: http
 s://db.seven-sages-of-rome.org/Main_Page. Participants are encouraged to b
 rouse the data base in preparation for the workshop. \n\nJutta Eming is a 
 professor of medieval German literature at the Institute for German and Ne
 therlandic Philology\, Freie Universität Berlin since 2010. From 2007-200
 9\, she was Assistant Professor in Medieval German Studies at UBC Vancouve
 r. \n\nRSVP for the Jutta Eming talk
END:VEVENT
