SUMMARY:[現場] Environmental Humanities Project: Public Lecture: “Ripp
 le Effects: Making with Rivers in War and Peace” with Amanda M. Smith
LOCATION:Building 260\, Pigott Hall\, Room 216
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/environmental-humanities-project-publ
 ic-lectureripple-effects-making-with-rivers-in-war-and-peace-with-amanda-m
 -smith
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/environmental-humanities-proj
 ect-public-lectureripple-effects-making-with-rivers-in-war-and-peace-with-
 amanda-m-smith\n\nDescription: \n\nBook Talk with Amanda Smith\n\n"Hydrofe
 minism posits that water is a life-giving body that permeates other bodies
  and blurs corporeal boundaries. According to this theory\, water is thus 
 a subject from which and with which to think. Building on this premise\, I
  suggest that if we are always thinking with water (Chen\, MacLeod\, Neima
 nis)\, then creative processes necessarily rely on aqueous ecologies while
  also interacting with and making them. Based on my forthcoming book\, The
  Nature of Conflict: Rivers in the Wake of the Colombian Peace Accords\, I
  examine how rivers in particular have participated in media that addresse
 s their place in Colombia's long and ongoing internal conflict. Tracing wh
 at I call "ripple effects"—aesthetic decisions\, procedures and effects 
 that register the material marks of a watershed's involvement in art-makin
 g—I consider the interdisciplinary Environmental Humanities methods requ
 ired to take water seriously as a creative subject. Ultimately\, I argue t
 hat creative practice involving bodies of water transforms media into an a
 rchive of fluvial subjectivity\, even if in a vestigial form\, offering a 
 lens with which to approach\, in this case\, the more-than-human impacts o
 f war."\n\nRSVP HERE For EHP Amanda Smith Event\n\nBio:\n\nAmanda M. Smith
  is Associate Professor of Latin American literature and culture at the Un
 iversity of California Santa Cruz and one of the co-editors of the Journal
  of Latin American Cultural Studies. Her research explores cultur
END:VEVENT
