SUMMARY:[線上] Pediatric Orthopedics and Sports Medicine Lecture Series: 
 Pediatric Lower Extremity Alignment and In-Toeing
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/pediatric-orthopedics-and-sports-medi
 cine-lecture-series-pediatric-lower-extremity-alignment-and-in-toeing
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/pediatric-orthopedics-and-spo
 rts-medicine-lecture-series-pediatric-lower-extremity-alignment-and-in-toe
 ing\n報名/票券: https://stanford.cloud-cme.com/course/courseoverview?P
 =5&EID=60458\n\nOverviewThis lecture will provide an overview of pediatric
  lower extremity development\, helping learners distinguish physiologic va
 riations from pathologic findings. Learners will review common causes of i
 n-toeing gait\, develop strategies for identifying the source of rotationa
 l deformities through physical examination\, and recognize red flags in ga
 it and lower extremity alignment. The session will also provide practical 
 guidance for counseling families about expected natural history and determ
 ining when further intervention is warranted.\n\nRegistrationRegistration 
 is required.\n\nAll practitioners: free.\n\nCreditsAMA PRA Category 1 Cred
 its™ (1.00 hours)\, BOC Category A Hours/CEUs (1.00 hours)\, Non-Physici
 an Participation Credit (1.00 hours)\, Occupational Therapy Contact Hours 
 (1.00 hours)\, Physical Therapy Contact Hours (1.00 hours)\n\nTarget Audie
 nceSpecialties - Orthopedics & Sports Medicine\, PediatricsProfessions - A
 thletic Trainer\, Fellow/Resident\, Non-Physician\, Occupational Therapist
 \, Physical Therapist\n ObjectivesAt the conclusion of this activity\, lea
 rners should be able to:\n1. Review pediatric lower extremity alignment an
 d differentiate physiologic from pathologic. \n2. Discuss the most common 
 causes of in-toeing gait in children and identify the source of rotational
  deformity through physical exam. \n3. Identify red flags in gait and lowe
 r extremity alignment. \n4. Counsel families on natural history and indica
 tions for further intervention. \n\nAccreditationIn support of imp
END:VEVENT
