DTSTAMP:20261027T123341Z
DTSTART:20261029T233000Z
DTEND:20261030T010000Z
SUMMARY:[現場]  A Conversation with Matthew L. Jones\, Princeton Univer
 sity\, on his book How Data Happened and the development of AI\,  with 
 comments by Tom Mullaney\, Stanford University
LOCATION:Building 200\, History Corner\, 303
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/a-conversation-withmatthew-l-jones-pr
 inceton-university-on-his-bookhow-data-happenedand-the-development-of-ai-w
 ith-comments-by-tom-mullaney-stanford-university
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/a-conversation-withmatthew-l-
 jones-princeton-university-on-his-bookhow-data-happenedand-the-development
 -of-ai-with-comments-by-tom-mullaney-stanford-university\n\nAbstract: From
  facial recognition—capable of checking people into flights or identifyi
 ng undocumented residents—to automated decision systems that inform who 
 gets loans and who receives bail\, each of us moves through a world determ
 ined by data-empowered algorithms. But these technologies didn’t just ap
 pear: they are part of a history that goes back centuries\, from the censu
 s enshrined in the US Constitution to the birth of eugenics in Victorian B
 ritain to the development of Google search. The book  illuminates the ways
  in which data has long been used as a tool and a weapon in arguing for wh
 at is true\, as well as a means of rearranging or defending power. It expl
 ores how data was created and curated\, as well as how new mathematical an
 d computational techniques developed to contend with that data serve to sh
 ape people\, ideas\, society\, military operations\, and economies. Althou
 gh technology and mathematics are at its heart\, the story of data ultimat
 ely concerns an unstable game among states\, corporations\, and people. Ho
 w were new technical and scientific capabilities developed\; who supported
 \, advanced\, or funded these capabilities or transitions\; and how did th
 ey change who could do what\, from what\, and to whom?  Wiggins and Jones 
 focus on these questions as they trace data’s historical arc\, and look 
 to the future. By understanding the trajectory of data—where it has been
  and where it might yet go—Wiggins and Jones argue that we can understan
 d how to bend it to ends that we col
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54242650519833@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T123341Z
DTSTART:20261029T190000Z
DTEND:20261029T203000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Comics: More Than Words: Hanako Asakura - Manga as (or in?
 ) Conversation:  History\, Fandom\, and the Classroom
LOCATION:Building 260\, Pigott Hall\, Rm 252
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/comics-more-than-words-hanako-asakura
 -manga-as-or-in-conversation
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/comics-more-than-words-hanako
 -asakura-manga-as-or-in-conversation\n\nPlease join Comics: More Than Word
 s for Manga as (or in?) Conversation: History\, Fandom\, and the Classroom
  a discussion with Hanako Asakura (San Francisco State University). The ta
 lk will focus on teaching manga as a medium that generates conversations a
 mong readers\, creators\, communities\, and the broader culture.\n\nDr. As
 akura is a lecturer in the Department of Humanities and Comparative World 
 Literature at San Francisco State University. Dr. Asakura was also part of
  the cultural programming related to The Art of Manga exhibition organized
  by the de Young Museum.\n\nIn preparation for the talk and the subsequent
  discussion\, please:\n 1) Read up to page 50 of Yoshihiro Tatsumi's A Dri
 fting Life.\n 2) RSVP and submit a question you would like to ask Dr. Asak
 ura here.
END:VEVENT
