SUMMARY:[現場] Artist Talk with CFGNY: A Practice in Collaboration
LOCATION:Anderson Collection\, The Denning Family Resource Center
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/artist-talk-with-cfgny-a-practice-in-
 collaboration
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/artist-talk-with-cfgny-a-prac
 tice-in-collaboration\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1F
 AIpQLSePvIBLF2R7SOI5P-HkmUzFG23gEpbrrmbqRCMWP9Q8SaT0OA/viewform?usp=header
 \n\nPlease join us for a roundtable discussion with CFGNY (Concept Foreign
  Garments New York) — an artist collective whose multidisciplinary pract
 ice combines image making\, installation\, performance and garment making 
 — moderated by artist Vincent Chong ('26). The collective uses materials
  such as porcelain\, stuffed animals\, photography\, drawing\, and corruga
 ted cardboard to investigate notions of mass production\, and fetishistic 
 desire. At the heart of these investigations\, Asian identity fractures an
 d fragments into what the collective refers to as\, “vaguely Asian.” \
 n\nRSVP HERE\n\nPanelists\n\nCFGNY is an artist collective whose research-
 based practice takes the form of image making\, installation\, sculpture\,
 garment making\, and performance to expand ideas of racialization and subj
 ectivity. Founded in 2016\, the collective continually returns to the term
  “vaguely Asian”: an understanding of racial identity as a specific cu
 ltural experience that includes being perceived as other.\n\nRecent an
 d upcoming exhibitions\, performances and projects include the 82nd Whit
 ney Biennial\, New York (2026)\; Contemporary Art Gallery\, Vancouver (2
 025)\; Cooper Hewitt Smithsonian Design Museum\, New York (2024)\; Ham
 mer Museum\, Los Angeles (2024)\; SculptureCenter\, New York (2023)\; 
 PIN-UP/Marséll\, Milan (2023)\; Japan Society\, New York (2022)\; X M
 useum\, Beijing (2021)\; RISD Museum\, Providence (2019)\;and Stedelij
 k Museum\, Amsterdam (2018). In October 2024\, CFGNY was named Frieze Lo
 ndon’s Focus Stand Prize winner 
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54251043963312@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T121815Z
DTSTART:20261027T190000Z
DTEND:20261027T203000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] materia Reading Session: Micropower and the Non-human
LOCATION:Building 260\, Pigott Hall\, 216
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/materia-the-reading-session-3585
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/materia-the-reading-session-3
 585\n\nWe are very pleased to invite you to materia's first event of the a
 cademic year: a very multidisciplinary Reading Session!\n\nThis reading se
 ssion will be an introduction to our yearly theme: Micropower and the Non-
 human. Micropower\, or the microphysics of power\, is a category establish
 ed by Foucault early on in his thinking\, before his turn towards biopolit
 ics. In Discipline and Punish (1975)\, Foucault posits that the microphysi
 cs of power’s “field of validity is situated in a sense between [state
  apparatuses] and the bodies themselves with their materiality and their f
 orces.” (26).If\, on the one hand\, we understand bodies in an expanded 
 sense\, without privileging the human\,and\, on the other\, we probe in de
  facto contemporary state apparatuses from the bottom up(algorithmic gover
 nance\, corporate takeover of attention span\, etc.)\, we find multiple op
 portunities to intervene. We would like to rekindle the spirit of the orig
 inal formulation\, revise Foucault\, and set him into conversation with di
 fferent archives\, in keeping with the group’s orientation\, Latin Ameri
 canist and otherwise. \n\nWe will read a selection that includes excerpts 
 from Foucault Discipline and Punish\, Jane Bennett's "The Force of Things"
 \,  an excerpt from Clarice Lispector's The Hour of the Star and a selecti
 on from Nerea Barjola Ramos' The Sexist Microphysics of Power. You can acc
 ess all texts and some related materials here.\n\nWe kindly ask you to foc
 us on Foucault's excerpt and read one of the additional text
END:VEVENT
