SUMMARY:[混合] Superintelligence or Super Stupidity? Designing AI Systems
  for Human Flourishing
LOCATION:Building 120\, McClatchy Hall\, Main Quad\, Studio S40 - bring you
 r Stanford ID card/mobile ID to enter the building
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/superintelligence-or-super-stupidity-
 designing-ai-systems-for-human-flourishing
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/superintelligence-or-super-st
 upidity-designing-ai-systems-for-human-flourishing\n線上連結: https://
 youtube.com/live/0FahuE94C3U?feature=share\n線上資訊:\nSign up for the
  webinar here: https://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_FhoD1uNcSEOf5
 dBb6-gZNg#/registration\n報名/票券: https://cvent.me/VE7w48\n\nJoin th
 e Tech Impact and Policy Center on November 3rd from 12PM–1PM Pacific fo
 r a seminar with Pat Pataranutaporn.\n\nStanford affiliates are invited to
  join us at 11:40 AM for lunch\, prior to the seminar.  The Fall Seminar S
 eries continues through December\; see our Fall Seminar Series page for sp
 eakers and topics. Sign up for our newsletter for announcements. \n\nAbout
  the Seminar:\n\nAs AI becomes woven into our daily lives and social insti
 tutions\, we are becoming “cyborgs\,” extending our minds and capabili
 ties through technology. But greater technological capability does not aut
 omatically lead to greater human flourishing. How can we design AI that st
 rengthens our ability to learn\, create\, connect\, and make thoughtful de
 cisions? In this talk\, I will introduce an interdisciplinary approach to 
 building AI systems and studying their effects on people. Through personal
 ized AI prototypes and large-scale experiments\, my research explores how 
 AI can both enhance and undermine human decision-making\, well-being\, rel
 ationships\, and sense of self. This work combines design\, engineering\, 
 and qualitative and quantitative research to advance a science of human-AI
  interaction grounded in human flourishing. I will also explore how resear
 ch can translate into real-world impact: from an AI digital twin that supp
 orts long-term thinking and has reached users in more than 100 countries\,
  to research that has helped shape legislation protecting children from co
 mpanion AI. Finally\, I will discuss our 
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54321714796042@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261027T123938Z
DTSTART:20261104T200000Z
DTEND:20261104T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Lunch Club Series | Navigating Heritage: Chinook Art and P
 ublic History Along the Columbia River
LOCATION:Building 500\, Archaeology Center\, 106
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/lunch-club-series-3721
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/lunch-club-series-3721\n線
 上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_-XB5tRt_TACr_ppYR5
 oBvQ\n\nLunch Club provides affiliates of the Stanford Archaeology Center 
 with a community-oriented forum for engagement with current issues in arch
 aeology. On Nov 4\, 2026\, we will host Dr.Jon Daehnke from University of 
 California\, Santa Cruz.\n\nAbstract: \n\nIn this talk I explore the conte
 sted nature of marking Indigenous history and continued presence/absence i
 n settler colonial states. Specifically\, I look at monuments\, memorials\
 , and other sites of public history and heritage along the Columbia River 
 that offer reminders of Indigenous – and in this case\, specifically Chi
 nook – occupation of the landscape. In part\, this work addresses the ch
 allenges that the Chinook Indian Nation faces regarding the public represe
 ntation of their own history\, especially at “official” sites of herit
 age. But centrally in the work I explore how the Chinook are responding to
 \, and actively shaping\, discussions and practice surrounding monuments\,
  memorials\, and sites of heritage. In part this is through increased part
 icipation in management of “official” sites of heritage. But it is als
 o connected to the role that the revitalization of Chinookan art has playe
 d in reclaiming heritage landscapes\, as well as opportunities for the Chi
 nook to tell their own stories on their own lands.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54321924632772@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261023T215459Z
DTSTART:20270224T200000Z
DTEND:20270224T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Distinguished Lecture Series Lunch Talk | From Plans to Pe
 ople: Rough-and-Ready Reconstructions of a Bronze Age Citadel
LOCATION:Building 500\, Archaeology Center\, 106
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/distinguished-lecture-series-lunch-ta
 lk-from-plans-to-people-rough-and-ready-reconstructions-of-a-bronze-age-ci
 tadel
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/distinguished-lecture-series-
 lunch-talk-from-plans-to-people-rough-and-ready-reconstructions-of-a-bronz
 e-age-citadel\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_
 O5L3NyY1RbezMITLQsAL6w\n\nEach quarter\, the Stanford Archaeology Center i
 nvites prominent archaeologists from around the globe to be in residence f
 or a week as a Distinguished Lecturer. During their residency\, the Distin
 guished Lecturer gives two lectures and interacts with faculty\, postdocto
 ral scholars and students. Stanford Archaeology Center will host Prof. Chr
 istopher H. Roosevelt from  Koc University over two days (Feb 24 and Feb 2
 5 for the Winter Quarter of this academic year. \n\n \n\nAbstract:\n \n\nV
 isual and numeric reconstructions of archaeological sites can become their
  most evocative and indelible portraits. A bird's-eye view or a population
  figure often conveys a palpable sense of an ancient community and outlive
 s the data used to produce it. Because such reconstructions are so easily 
 convincing and lasting\, their resolution should match that of their under
 lying datasets. Yet the history of archaeology\, from Çatalhöyük to Tro
 y and beyond\, is rife with disputed reconstructions and corrected populat
 ion estimates. We should question not how accurate such reconstructions mu
 st be\, but what resolution the evidence can honestly support.\n\nUsing re
 cent work of the Kaymakçı Archaeological Project in western Anatolia\, t
 his lecture presents a combined approach to visual and numerical reconstru
 ctions of a second-millennium BCE citadel and its population that privileg
 es the spatial and chronological resolution of its datasets over the photo
 realism or exactitude of its outputs. Aerial and ground-based remote
END:VEVENT
