SUMMARY:[線上] The Spiritual Highway: Religious World Making in Megacity 
 Lagos - Professor Marloes Janson
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-spiritual-highway-religious-world
 -making-in-megacity-lagos-professor-marloes-janson
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-spiritual-highway-religio
 us-world-making-in-megacity-lagos-professor-marloes-janson\n報名/票券:
  https://forms.gle/H5CFNgAxdF3wekwE6\n\nSession Description:\n\nOpened in 
 1978 at the height of Nigeria’s oil boom\, the Lagos-Ibadan Expressway w
 as once celebrated as a symbol of progress. However\, rising traffic volum
 es and decades of neglect gradually transformed it into one of the country
 ’s deadliest roads\, earning it the nickname “Highway of Death”. Yet
  even as it declined as a transport corridor\, it flourished as a stage fo
 r public religiosity. Today\, lined with vast prayer camps\, it is widely 
 known as the “Spiritual Highway”. \n\nIn this panel discussion\, photo
 grapher Dr Akintunde Akinleye and anthropologist Professor Marloes Janson 
 will explore how religious organizations have emerged as influential city-
 builders in Lagos\, Nigeria’s economic hub. Drawing on ethnographic rese
 arch and photography\, they will show how the Lagos-Ibadan Expressway has 
 become a key site for understanding the relationship between religion and 
 urban development. Along this corridor\, religion is not simply a feature 
 of urban life\; it is a constitutive force through which the city itself i
 s made and remade.\n\nSpeaker Biography:\n\nMarloes Janson is Professor of
  West African Anthropology at SOAS University of London. She holds a PhD i
 n Anthropology from Leiden University\, The Netherlands. Her publications 
 include Islam\, Youth\, and Modernity in the Gambia: The Tablighi Jamaʻat
  (Cambridge University Press\, 2014)\, which has been awarded the RAI Amau
 ry Talbot Prize for African Anthropology\; Crossing Religious Boundaries: 
 Islam\, Christianity\, and ‘
END:VEVENT
