SUMMARY:[現場] Claudia Hart: Techno-ethics and the Sociology of Simulatio
 n
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/claudia-hart-techno-ethics-and-the-so
 ciology-of-simulation
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/claudia-hart-techno-ethics-an
 d-the-sociology-of-simulation\n報名/票券: https://sls-claudia-hart.eve
 ntbrite.com\n\nI was early into simulations - which isolated me because I 
 found myself inside one of the most male-dominated spaces in contemporary 
 culture: the world of simulation technologies. Its denizens were mostly ex
 -military\, its technologies built to visualize war games. When I started 
 in 1990\, its culture seemed devoid of women. My response was to build a f
 eminist practice inside of that militarized space. My practice is trans-me
 dia and spans 3D animation\, virtual and augmented reality\, performance\,
  illustrated books\, and installation. I appropriate Western canonical fig
 ures — philosophers\, poets\, architects\, painters — and reinvent the
 ir dense and sober languages as theatrical\, often dark fantasy worlds. Bu
 t the rooms I build echo with women’s voices: voices that I also perform
 \, script\, and digitally transform. I am mutable and so are computer file
 s — a perfect fit! My themes are serious (techno-ethics\, the sociology 
 of simulation\, the politics of embodiment)\, although my methodology is t
 heatrical and allegorical.\n\nOriginally trained in art and architectural 
 history at New York University and Columbia University\, Claudia Hart bega
 n her career in the New York City art scene before transitioning to digita
 l media in the late 1990s. Her historical training remains foundational to
  her work. Rather than treating technology as detached from the past\, Har
 t situates digital simulation within a long continuum of representation ex
 tending from Renaissance perspective and photography to virtua
END:VEVENT
